{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"text\\\"]) for x in a[15:25]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"text\\\"]) for x in a[4:15]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"text\\\"]) for x in a[4:15]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### 5 2026-09-22 Sea to adopt Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform; more Southeast Asian firms build with Nemotron \n Sea to adopt Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform; more Southeast Asian firms build with Nemotron\nSea will use Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform to scale AI across Shopee, Monee and Garena, while organisations in Southeast Asia build applications with Nvidia's Nemotron models.\nSingapore-founded Sea will adopt Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform to build and deploy artificial-intelligence (AI) models and agents at greater scale across its Shopee, Monee and Garena businesses.\nVera Rubin is a computing platform that provides the processing capacity to develop and run AI systems. Sea says it will use Vera Rubin to extend AI work already underway at its businesses.\nAt Shopee, Compass is an in-house AI model built and run on Nvidia graphics processors and software. It powers search, product recommendations, fraud checks and customer service.\nOver eight months, Compass's monthly processing volume rose from 3 billion to 340 billion tokens. Tokens are the units of text an AI model reads and generates. Compass now accounts for most of Shopee's AI traffic. It has also made fraud detection 50 times more efficient than manual review and cut processing costs by 90%.\nBeyond Compass, Shopee uses AI to help sellers create product listings, improve product discovery, and engage customers.\nMeanwhile, Sea's digital-financial-services division Monee uses AI to detect fraud and assess credit risk. As for Garena, it uses AI to improve gaming experiences and support safe, inclusive online spaces.\nAnother Nvidia offering seeing an uptake in Southeast Asia is Nemotron, which is a collection of open AI models, datasets and software tools that organisations can customise for their own languages and business needs.\nFor instance, NCS has adopted Nemotron models and its video-search-and-summarisation blueprint for enterprise and public-sector work. NCS is also developing physical-AI applications for humanoid robotics.\nAt ST Engineering, Nvidia's NeMo tools and cuOpt software are being used to develop its AI Studio platform and deploy AI applications in areas including marine maintenance, repair and overhaul.\nMeanwhile, Singapore's Home Team Science and Technology Agency (HTX) is beginning research using two Nemotron models for public-safety applications. One model could support reasoning and agentic workflows, while the other combines vision, audio and language capabilities for applications based on operational data.\nOther projects focus on adapting AI for local languages and legal systems. This includes AI Singapore adding Nemotron open models and NeMo tools to SEA-LION, its model family for Southeast Asian languages and cultures.\nIn Vietnam, Viettel AI has fine-tuned Nemotron 3 Super for Vietnamese-language and agentic applications. The model is set to be used in Legal AI, an agent system for Viettel Group employees and external customers.\nMeanwhile, Thailand's iApp Technology is adapting Nemotron 3 Nano for Thai-language legal work. Nvidia says the resulting model powers Thanoy, iApp's legal-assistant chatbot, which serves about 43,000 users.\nNvidia also cites smart-city projects in the region. Malaysia's ITMAX is applying Nvidia software to traffic operations, while Thailand's AS-TECH is using it to improve passenger flow in airports.\n\n### 6 2026-09-23 Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now? \n Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\n\n### 7 2026-09-23 Nvidia Slips as Four Macs Attack Cloud Inference Economics \n Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI chip and data center leader, faces a challenge at the smaller end of the AI market: Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) is pitching Macs that run demanding models in an office, without a cloud bill for every token. Nvidia shares slipped nearly 1.3% to $227.98 Wednesday morning.\nApple demonstrated four connected Mac Studios running a trillion-parameter model to fix a coding bug. The pitch is simple: buy the machines once, then keep using them. That could appeal to businesses with steady workloads or sensitive data. Nvidia's latest quarter puts the scale of the contest in perspective: data centers generated $89 billion of its $96.2 billion in revenue, or roughly 92.5%.\nThe GF Score chart gives Nvidia 95 out of 100. Profitability and growth are its strongest points; GF Value is visibly weaker. Apple's desktop pitch is unlikely to displace the large systems used to train frontier models. The question for investors is narrower: how much routine AI work can move onto machines customers own before it reaches a cloud GPU?\n\n### 8 2026-09-23 Nvidia establishes Israeli research group focused on AI for medicine and science \n Nvidia establishes Israeli research group focused on AI for medicine and science\nThe Applied AI Architecture group has begun recruiting researchers in Israel and will work on AI applications ranging from genomics and drug discovery to new materials.\nA new Nvidia research group focused on using artificial intelligence to accelerate medical and scientific breakthroughs has begun operating in Israel, Calcalist has learned.\nThe group, called Applied AI Architecture, is based at Nvidia’s Israeli development center and operates within the company’s global CTO office. The office is led by CTO Michael Kagan, a co-founder of Mellanox who is currently considered the most senior Israeli executive at Nvidia reporting directly to founder and CEO Jensen Huang.\nThe group currently employs several researchers in Israel, with Nvidia planning to recruit dozens more over the coming year. The company’s website already lists several research positions in fields related to the group at its Tel Aviv offices.\nApplied AI Architecture is headed by Dr. Nati Daniel, who holds a PhD in artificial intelligence from the Technion and previously held senior positions at Intel, GE and Huawei.\nThe group is being built around a long-term goal: using AI not simply to automate existing processes, but to accelerate the pace of scientific and medical discovery. Rather than focusing on immediate commercial applications, its researchers will work on scientific concepts that could eventually mature into technologies incorporated into Nvidia’s systems.\nNvidia already operates research groups in several locations around the world. What distinguishes the Israeli initiative is its placement within the global CTO office, as well as its model of combining in-house research with collaborations with medical institutions.\nIsrael was chosen in part because of its concentration of advanced AI and medical research centers. Nvidia is already developing partnerships in the country and is expected to announce additional collaborations.\nOne of the group's existing projects is a collaboration with Sheba Medical Center and Mount Sinai Hospital in New York to develop a Genomic Foundation Model, or gFM. The model is intended to help researchers study the roughly 98% of the human genome that has yet to be deeply explored and about which scientific knowledge remains limited.\nThe project aims to identify new approaches to disease prevention, improve diagnostics and advance personalized medicine.\nThe Israeli group is also participating in Nvidia’s global collaboration with pharmaceutical company Eli Lilly. The two companies plan to invest approximately $1 billion over five years to use AI to accelerate drug discovery and development by integrating and analyzing genomic and sequencing data alongside chemistry, biology and cellular context.\nThe Israeli team is participating in the development of AI technologies intended to shorten the path from scientific research to the development of new medicines and treatments.\nAnother project involving the group is GENMAT, an international research initiative launched several months ago that received a grant of approximately €6 million from the European Union’s Horizon program. The project brings together companies, universities and research institutes from across Europe to develop AI tools for discovering and developing new materials, including materials for the aviation, automotive and pharmaceutical industries.\nThe creation of the group adds another layer to Nvidia’s rapidly expanding Israeli operations. The company’s local development center has increasingly taken on a central role in its global research and development activities, while Nvidia continues to expand its workforce and operations in Israel.\nThe expansion could also have broader implications for Israel. Nvidia’s growing presence in the country has the potential to affect exports, GDP, tax revenues and government finances, particularly if the company continues to expand its Israeli operations at its current pace.\nWith Nvidia forecasting continued strong growth for the remainder of the year, the latest research expansion suggests that the company’s investment in Israel may still have further to run.\n\n### 9 2026-09-23 Nvidia Just Qualified Tesla and Vertiv Products for Its New AI-Factory Standard \n Nvidia is extending its influence beyond GPUs into the electrical and cooling equipment that determines whether an AI data center can actually run. On September 21, the company introduced its DSX Ready program and named Tesla, Inc. (NASDAQ: TSLA ) among qualified battery-energy-storage suppliers and Vertiv Holdings Co (NYSE: VRT ) among qualified cooling suppliers. NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is effectively publishing a compatibility layer for the physical AI factory, which can shorten design work for developers while potentially increasing Nvidia's influence over what gets specified around its racks.\nNvidia is standardizing the parts around the GPU\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) wins if DSX Ready reduces friction in deploying dense accelerated-compute systems. Power stability, backup, liquid cooling and controls are no longer side issues when a rack can consume hundreds of kilowatts. A recognized supplier list can help projects move faster and make Nvidia's reference designs stickier. The bear case is that qualification is not a guarantee of project wins. Nvidia itself says DSX Ready does not replace site-level engineering or imply site-level stability.\nFor Tesla, Inc. (NASDAQ:TSLA), the program puts Megapack directly into the AI-infrastructure conversation. Battery systems can smooth power, provide ride-through capability and support sites waiting on grid upgrades. That opens another demand pool for Tesla Energy beyond utilities and renewable projects. The risk is economics: data-center developers may prefer other battery vendors, and storage revenue still has to compete for capital inside a company valued primarily on vehicles, autonomy and robotics.\nVertiv gets the densest part of the problem\nVertiv Holdings Co (NYSE:VRT) has the most direct operating exposure because Nvidia qualified its 2.3-megawatt CoolChip coolant distribution unit for the program. Higher rack density raises cooling content per megawatt, giving Vertiv a path to grow faster than the data-center footprint itself. The counterweight is expectations. Vertiv has already rerated around AI demand, so qualification matters most if it converts into orders and margins rather than becoming a logo on a standards page.\nInsider Monkey's Q2 database showed 285 hedge funds holding Nvidia, up from 275 in Q1; Fisher Asset Management raised its stake about 3%. Tesla fell to 116 hedge-fund holders from 123, while BAMCO held 12.52 million shares after increasing its position 5%. Vertiv rose to about 112 hedge-fund holders from 96 in Q1; AQR Capital Management held 2.69 million shares after increasing its stake roughly 1%. All three filings predate the DSX announcement.\nThe program's significance is not that Nvidia suddenly owns the power train of a data center. It is that the company is defining what a deployable Nvidia factory should look like. Tesla and Vertiv can benefit if customers treat qualification as a shortcut. Nvidia benefits if every shortcut leads back to its architecture.\nWhile we acknowledge the potential of NVDA, TSLA and VRT as investments, we believe certain other AI stocks offer greater upside potential and carry less downside risk. If you're looking for an extremely undervalued AI stock that also stands to benefit significantly from Trump-era tariffs and the onshoring trend, see our free report on the best short-term AI stock .\n\n### 10 2026-09-23 NVIDIA Smooth Motion Now Runs on RTX 30 Series GPUs Thanks to NVSmooth30 Mod, RTX 3050 Laptop and RTX 3080 Confirmed Working \n NVIDIA's latest frame generation technologies continue to make their way onto older RTX GPUs thanks to the modding community. This time, developer Adeline released NVSmooth30 , an experimental compatibility mod that enables NVIDIA Smooth Motion on GeForce RTX 30 Series graphics cards, which the feature officially doesn't support.\nIntroduced with the RTX 50 Series and subsequently expanded to RTX 40 Series GPUs , Smooth Motion is NVIDIA's driver-level AI frame generation solution. Unlike DLSS Frame Generation, it does not require a game to integrate NVIDIA's SDK, allowing users to generate one additional frame for each rendered one in compatible titles that lack native frame generation suppor t.\nLike AMD Fluid Motion Frames 2.1 , NVIDIA Smooth Motion is a driver-level technology that generates additional frames between traditionally rendered ones to improve perceived smoothness and increase displayed frame rates, even in games that lack native frame generation support.\nNVSmooth30 works by modifying NVIDIA's NvPresent64.dll runtime behavior, bypassing the GPU architecture and capability checks that prevent Smooth Motion from initializing on Ampere hardware. The mod also intercepts the CUDA module-loading process, retargeting compatible Ada Lovelace ( SM89 ) GPU code to Ampere (SM86). According to Adeline , the implementation does not redistribute NVIDIA's proprietary binaries and instead uses the NvPresent64.dll version installed with the user's graphics driver.\nThe developer initially demonstrated the project on an RTX 3050 Laptop GPU, but other users have already reported success with considerably more powerful Ampere GPUs .\nOne Reddit user confirmed that Smooth Motion works in Kingdom Come: Deliverance II on an RTX 3070, while another reported that their RTX 3080 achieved approximately 130 displayed FPS, up from 65 FPS without frame generation in the same game . These are preliminary user-reported results rather than independently verified benchmarks, but they suggest that compatibility extends beyond the developer's original test system.\nAdeline has also been working on an experimental Direct3D 11-to-Direct3D 12 presentation bridge , allowing NVIDIA's presentation layer to wrap a compatible D3D12 swap chain when running older D3D11 games. This could potentially expand the mod's usefulness across a much larger library of PC titles.\nGetting NVSmooth30 running involves placing its version.dll proxy file alongside the game's executable and enabling Smooth Motion for that title through NVIDIA Profile Inspector . The developer notes that the setting may need to be reactivated following graphics driver updates.\nThere are, however, some important caveats. NVSmooth30 remains an alpha-stage project, and compatibility across different games, drivers, and RTX 30 Series GPUs is not guaranteed . NVIDIA driver updates could also introduce changes that break the mod's functionality. Users are advised against trying it in competitive or anti-cheat-protected games, while crashes, corrupted frames, and black screens remain possible .\nWith the recent emergence of mods enabling DLSS Frame Generation and Multi Frame Generation on older GeForce RTX GPUs, NVSmooth30 represents another interesting development for Ampere owners who want to experiment with NVIDIA's newer rendering technologies without upgrading their graphics cards.\nThe project's source code and installation instructions are available on GitHub , while additional demonstrations and early user impressions can be found in Adeline 's original Reddit thread .\n\n### 11 2026-09-23 Apple Takes AI Fight Straight to Nvidia and Microsoft \n Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) is making an unusually aggressive push into enterprise artificial intelligence , pitching its newest high-end Macs as an alternative to the expensive cloud infrastructure that has helped Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) and Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ) dominate the AI boom.\nThe argument is largely about economics.\nApple says businesses can run increasingly sophisticated AI workloads locally on Macs instead of continually paying cloud providers or AI developers for computing capacity measured in tokens. Its upgraded Mac mini and Mac Studio systems are aimed at tasks including software development and complex business workflows. Some configurations can approach $20,000.\nApple demonstrated just how far it wants to push that idea at its latest launch event.\nThe company connected four Mac Studios and used them to run a trillion-parameter AI model that identified and fixed a graphics coding problem, a workload that would ordinarily require data-center infrastructure. The four-machine setup operated from a single wall outlet.\nApple believes its unified-memory architecture and focus on power efficiency give Macs an advantage for running large models locally. The same architecture can also allow developers to move workloads across devices ranging from high-end Mac Studios to iPhones and iPads.\nBut Apple is attacking from far behind.\nIDC estimates Macs hold only about 4.6% of the enterprise desktop market, compared with 91.3% for Microsoft Windows. Nvidia, meanwhile, remains dominant in data-center AI and is working with PC manufacturers to expand AI capabilities on Windows machines.\nApple does not need to replace Nvidia data centers or Windows PCs for this strategy to matter. The opportunity is to establish Macs as a credible middle ground between conventional corporate PCs and expensive cloud AI infrastructure. Investors should watch enterprise Mac adoption, demand for high-memory configurations, developer support and whether local inference becomes economically attractive enough to shift some workloads away from the cloud. Success could give Apple a larger role in enterprise AI while strengthening the Mac business beyond its traditional creative-user base.\n\n### 12 2026-09-23 IONQ Stock On Track To Hit Over 1-Month High On Nvidia Superion 256 Deployment, Quantum Error Decoder Breakthrough \n / IONQ Stock On Track To Hit Over 1-Month High On Nvidia Superion 256 Deployment, Quantum Error Decoder Breakthrough\nIONQ Stock On Track To Hit Over 1-Month High On Nvidia Superion 256 Deployment, Quantum Error Decoder Breakthrough\nIonQ’s Superion 256 system is scheduled for installation next year at Nvidia’s center.\nThe IonQ-NVIDIA research program will focus on integrating quantum processors with AI and supercomputing infrastructure.\nThe announcement comes just a day after IonQ announced its successful test of a real-time quantum error decoder.\nIonQ (IONQ) shares jumped in pre-market trade on Wednesday after the company said it successfully tested its first real-time quantum error decoder and announced that its Superion 256 system will become the first on-premises quantum processor deployed at Nvidia’s Accelerated Quantum Research Center.\nIONQ stock gained nearly 11% in the pre-market trade, on track to hit an over-one-month high, and was among the top-trending tickers on Stocktwits at the time of writing. www.tradingview.com/chart/5MigAogM\nRetail sentiment around IonQ improved to ‘bullish’ from ‘neutral’ over the past day.\nThe developments mark another step in IonQ’s push toward fault-tolerant quantum computing and deeper integration between quantum processors and conventional AI supercomputing infrastructure.\nIonQ To Deploy Superion 256 At Nvidia Center\nIonQ said on Wednesday that its Superion 256 will be installed at Nvidia’s Accelerated Quantum Research Center next year, where it will be linked directly to an Nvidia GB200 NVL72 system.\nThe companies will use Nvidia’s NVQLink technology and CUDA-Q platform to connect the quantum processor with Nvidia’s accelerated computing infrastructure. The research program will focus on hybrid quantum-classical software and large-scale system prototypes.\nNvidia said IonQ’s system will help researchers explore how quantum processors can be integrated with AI infrastructure and conventional supercomputing systems.\nNVDA stock edged 0.5% lower in pre-market trade on Wednesday amid broader market weakness. Retail sentiment around the Jensen Huang-led AI bellwether has trended toward ‘bearish’ territory over the past three months.\nQuantum Computing Targets Real-World Applications\nThe partnership will also explore potential applications in financial services, including portfolio optimization and risk modeling, as well as in materials science and computational chemistry for drug discovery.\nIonQ and Nvidia previously worked with Oak Ridge National Laboratory and the University of Tennessee on research combining generative AI with distributed quantum algorithms.\nIonQ Tests Real-Time Quantum Error Decoder\nIonQ’s team-up with Nvidia comes less than a day after the company announced its successful test of a real-time quantum error decoder. It said that the milestone represents a key step toward correcting errors generated during quantum computations, an important requirement for building fault-tolerant quantum systems.\nThe company is targeting a roadmap toward 10,000 qubits and full fault tolerance through its Superion platform. IonQ introduced the sixth-generation Superion product line earlier this month, with the Superion 256 system available to order and first customer deliveries expected in 2027.\nIONQ stock has fallen over 11% year-to-date and over 40% in the last 12 months.\n\n### 13 2026-09-24 I'm Buying More Nvidia Stock Right Now. Here's Why. \n Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has grown into the world's largest company, with a market cap of more than $5 trillion. Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the gold standard for training and running high-level computing and artificial intelligence programs.\nSome people may, however, look at Nvidia's stock price and think that the shine is coming off Jensen Huang's company. Shares are up 21% so far this year, which is fine, but that's not even close to the 239% gain in 2023, the 171% jump in 2024, or even last year's 39% increase. Meanwhile, stocks like Sandisk , Micron , and Intel are all up more than 200% this year and have become Wall Street darlings.\nAre Nvidia's best days in the rearview mirror? Absolutely not. While the chipmaker may not replicate its massive 2023 gain, Nvidia remains an incredibly profitable company whose growth story is far from over. In fact, I think Nvidia is going to be putting up some wild numbers in the next two or three years, and that's why I'll be buying more Nvidia shares .\nNvidia is still growing fast, despite its size\nWhat stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\nNet income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\n\"We're the only company in the world that creates and builds, offers an entire AI factory platform, a full-stack system. And customers can still mix and match,\" CEO Jensen Huang said. \"However, most companies just don't have the skills to do that or desire to do that. And so, there's an entire part of the market that we experience growth. There's sovereign AI, there're regional AIs, there're neoclouds, there're AI start-ups, there're enterprises, where we're seeing -- which represents about half of our business, and that's growing 100% a year.\"\nNvidia's new product line is just getting started\nNvidia's Hopper and Blackwell architectures have been enormously popular among hyperscalers and neoclouds building computing capacity to meet the growing demand for AI. But the company's Vera Rubin architecture, which uses NVLink chip-to-chip interconnects so its Rubin GPUs and Vera central processing units (CPUs) can share memory and improve efficiency, generates even more revenue. CFO Colette Kress said the Vera Rubin -- now in full production -- and full-stack AI platform has expanded the company's total addressable market from a range of $18 billion to $25 billion per gigawatt from Blackwell to $40 billion from Vera Rubin.\nThat's one of the reasons why Huang has been so confident in his prediction -- which he made last year and recently reiterated -- that AI infrastructure spending would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. And speaking to reporters last week while in Scotland, Huang suggested that Nvidia would double its chip sales in the coming year.\nIt's hard to get a group of people to agree on anything -- that's just human nature -- but Wall Street experts are nearly unanimous on Nvidia stock. Analysts at Rosenblatt have a \"Buy\" recommendation and a $390 price target, representing potential upside of 73%. Bernstein's analysts have a $400 price target and an \"Outperform\" rating, and Raymond James analysts have a whopping $515 price target and a \"Strong Buy\" rating.\nNvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year. And more of the same is expected for next year, following the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\n\n### 14 2026-09-24 Efficient MoE Training for Biological Foundation Models | NVIDIA Technical Blog \n By Faradawn Yang , Peter St John , Kyle Tretina and Zoey Zhang\nMixture-of-experts architectures scale model capacity more efficiently than dense transformers by activating only a subset of subnetworks for each token.\nThe TE Sequential API fuses GroupedLinear, ScaledSwiGLU, and routing-weight scaling into a ForwardGroupedMLP_CuTeGEMMSwiGLU_MXFP8 kernel, avoiding intermediate materialization.\nIn a training benchmark on eight NVIDIA B200 Tensor Core GPUs, the BioNeMo recipe delivered up to 2.21x the throughput of the Hugging Face baseline.\nAs language models grow, scaling dense architectures becomes increasingly expensive. In a dense transformer, every token passes through every layer, so adding capabilities increases computation for both training and inference.\nMixture-of-experts (MoE) architectures take a different approach to scaling by using many subnetworks, or experts, while activating only a small subset for each token.\nThis tradeoff has made MoE architectures increasingly attractive to the large language model (LLM) community. They can scale model capacity more efficiently, but the benefits depend heavily on implementation. Fragmented expert computation can reduce GPU utilization. Routing adds communication overhead, and larger parameter footprints create memory and distributed-training challenges. NVIDIA Transformer Engine (TE) helps address these bottlenecks with optimized primitives for grouped expert computation, kernel fusion, and low-precision training. As biological foundation models grow in parameter count and sequence length, these primitives can improve GPU efficiency while expanding model capacity.\nThis tutorial shows how to put these techniques into practice with the NVIDIA BioNeMo MoE recipe and TE. You’ll see how GroupedLinear improves expert computation, MXFP8 reduces memory use, and the GroupedMLP kernel fuses quantization, SwiGLU, and routing-weight scaling. Together, these capabilities provide a practical reference for efficiently training MoE-based biological foundation models.\nBefore you begin, you need:\nFamiliarity with Python, PyTorch, and distributed training concepts\nAn NVIDIA CUDA-enabled environment—you can use the linked Dockerfile or install the recipe requirements\nAt least two GPUs for expert parallelism; NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs are required to use the fused MXFP8 GroupedMLP kernel\nChallenge 1: Fragmented expert kernels\nMoE models replace a single dense feed-forward block with multiple expert networks. A naive implementation, however, can trigger excessive kernel launches. For example, the Hugging Face baseline implementation iterates over all experts in a Python loop, with each expert triggering separate kernel launches.\nfor expert_idx, expert_layer in enumerate(self.experts):\nidx, top_x = torch.where(expert_mask[expert_idx])\ncurrent_state = hidden_states[None, top_x].reshape(-1, hidden_dim)\ncurrent_hidden = expert_layer(current_state) * routing_weights[top_x, idx, None]\nfinal_hidden_states.index_add_(0, top_x, current_hidden)\nGrouped execution preserves the individual expert matrices but submits their work together. TE’s GroupedLinear applies multiple linear transformations in one call by gathering the expert weights and input tokens. Because each expert can receive a different number of tokens, GroupedLinear accepts per-expert token counts ( split_sizes ). It submits the local experts through the TE grouped GEMM path instead of launching one PyTorch Linear operation per expert, reducing launch and scheduling overhead.\nUse GroupedLinear as follows. Each expert retains its own weight tensor ( weight0 , weight1 , and so on), and the call accepts the per-expert token counts as an additional positional argument:\nout_features=2 * intermediate_size,\ndtype=torch.bfloat16,\ngate_up_output = experts_gate_up(tokens, split_sizes)\nCompared with the Python loop, this approach submits the gate-up projections as one grouped operation instead of multiple separate calls.\nHugging Face Transformers also provides grouped_mm . However, TE can fuse GroupedLinear with MXFP8 quantization, activation, routing-weight scaling, and intermediate data movement into a GroupedMLP kernel, as shown in the later sections.\nChallenge 2: Large model size and activation memory\nMoE architectures increase total parameter capacity, and genomics workloads often use long sequences, which puts pressure on activation memory during training. BF16 uses 16 bits to represent each model weight and activation.\nThe BioNeMo recipe uses TE to support FP8 and MXFP8 training, reducing memory use. Both formats represent weight and activation values with 8 bits instead of 16. The main difference between FP8 and MXFP8 is scaling granularity: MXFP8 assigns a scaling factor to each block of 32 consecutive values, helping preserve numerical range and accuracy. On NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs, MXFP8 is hardware-accelerated, enabling MXFP8 GEMMs to use specialized Tensor Core instructions. For details about MXFP8 and block scaling, see the Transformer Engine FP8 primer .\nChallenge 3: Quantization overhead in low-precision training\nAlthough most training computation uses 8-bit precision, the model retains its master weights in 16 bits. The training framework therefore adds quantization and dequantization steps to convert between formats. Quantization converts BF16 weights and activations to MXFP8 before the low-precision GEMM; dequantization converts the result back to the higher-precision format. A naive path performs these steps as separate operations, motivating the fused MLP path described next.\nfp8_recipe = te_recipe.MXFP8BlockScaling()\nmodel = TEMixtralMXFP8ForCausalLM(config, fp8_recipe=fp8_recipe, dispatcher=dispatcher)\nThe TE autocast API enables MXFP8 precision for the model’s forward and backward passes:\nwith te.autocast(enabled=True, recipe=self._fp8_recipe):\nfor decoder_layer in self.layers:\nhidden_states = decoder_layer(hidden_states)\nSee the BioNeMo recipe for the complete code.\nTo use the fused MLP, import the Transformer Engine Sequential API to chain together gate_up , ScaledSwiGLU , and down . The API also folds dequantization into the fused path. ScaledSwiGLU combines the routing probabilities (“scales”) with the expert feed-forward network computations.\nfrom transformer_engine.pytorch.ops import GroupedLinear, ScaledSwiGLU, Sequential\nexperts_ffn = Sequential(GroupedLinear(gate_up), ScaledSwiGLU(), GroupedLinear(down))\nThe TE Sequential API scans the operations and, when the pattern matches, replaces the GroupedLinear → ScaledSwiGLU → GroupedLinear sequence with a fused operation object: ForwardGroupedMLP_CuTeGEMMSwiGLU_MXFP8 for the forward pass and a matching fused backward operation. This reduces framework overhead, fuses the SwiGLU and probability-scaling work into the grouped MLP path, and avoids materializing some intermediate results.\nThese are several of the optimizations in the BioNeMo recipe. In our training benchmark on eight NVIDIA B200 Tensor Core GPUs, the recipe delivered up to 2.21x the throughput of the Hugging Face baseline.\nStart with the two-GPU L0_sanity configuration to confirm that expert parallelism and the training environment work correctly:\ntorchrun --nproc_per_node=2 train_fsdp2_ep.py --config-name L0_sanity\nAfter validation, scale to the Mixtral-8x7B configuration with expert parallelism (EP=8) and MXFP8 precision across eight GPUs:\ntorchrun --nproc_per_node=8 train_fsdp2_ep.py --config-name L1_8x7B_ep checkpoint.ckpt_dir=/path/to/ckpt\nSelect BF16 or MXFP8 based on your GPU and memory requirements and set the data-parallel and expert-parallel sizes so their product equals the total GPU count. The recipe README includes launch, checkpoint, and benchmark commands.\nTry the Mixtral Native Transformer Engine recipe in BioNeMo Recipes and learn more about the optimized MoE kernels in the NVIDIA Transformer Engine documentation .\nSudhakar Singh, Varun Thumbe, Santosh Santosh, Timur Rvachov, and Chris Hoge.\nData Center / Cloud | Data Science | Simulation / Modeling / Design | Healthcare & Life Sciences | HPC / Scientific Computing | BioNeMo | CUDA | Intermediate Technical | Tutorial | Drug Discovery | featured | Mixture of Experts (MoE)\nFaradawn Yang is an engineer on the AI platform software team at NVIDIA, where he focuses on AI inference products. Faradawn holds a master’s degree in computer science and a bachelor’s degree in mathematics and computer science from the University of Chicago. Before joining NVIDIA, he worked as a data engineer at a marketing measurement company.\nPeter is currently a machine learning engineer working on the BioNeMo team at NVIDIA, where he is working on accelerating digital biology workflows in the protein and genomics space. Prior to joining NVIDIA in 2022, he worked for 7 years as a project lead at the National Renewable Energy Lab, where his research investigated new fuel chemistries and microbial biomass conversion. He received his PhD in Chemical Engineering from UCSB in 2015.\nZoey Zhang is the product manager for AI training in Digital Biology at NVIDIA. Her background spans software engineering and machine learning research roles, with a degree in Biomedical Engineering from the University of Waterloo specializing in Medical AI and Computing. Zoey is passionate about accelerating scientific discovery and the development of life-saving treatments through AI and accelerated computing.\n\n### 15 2026-09-24 How IonQ’s On‑Prem Quantum System At NVIDIA (NVDA) Has Changed Its Investment Story \n In recent days, IonQ announced it will deploy its Superion 256 quantum computer as the first on‑premise system at NVIDIA's Accelerated Quantum Research Center, directly linking quantum hardware to NVIDIA GB200 NVL72 infrastructure via NVQLink and CUDA‑Q for hybrid AI and quantum workloads.\nThis collaboration positions NVIDIA's platform at the center of early quantum‑AI co‑design, extending its role from powering today's AI data centers to shaping how future quantum and GPU systems work together across fields like finance, chemistry, and materials science.\nWe'll now examine how this deeper quantum‑AI integration with IonQ could influence NVIDIA's existing investment narrative around long‑term AI infrastructure leadership.\nRare earth metals are an input to most high-tech devices, military and defence systems and electric vehicles. The global race is on to secure supply of these critical minerals. Beat the pack to uncover the 36 best rare earth metal stocks of the very few that mine this essential strategic resource.\nTo own NVIDIA today, you need to believe that its full stack of GPUs, networking, and software will remain the default infrastructure for large scale AI, even as customers experiment with alternatives and export rules evolve. The IonQ Superion 256 deployment at NVIDIA's Accelerated Quantum Research Center is exciting, but it does not change the near term catalyst, which still centers on data center AI demand versus export and regulatory risk around China.\nAmong the recent announcements, the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance with Emerald AI and Google stands out here. If AI data centers can become more \"grid friendly\" and flex their power use, that may help ease one of NVIDIA's key risks around data center energy constraints and policy pushback, supporting the thesis that AI infrastructure build outs can continue to scale from here.\nYet even with all this momentum, investors should not ignore how quickly export controls or hyperscaler chip in sourcing could reshape NVIDIA's outlook...\nRead the full narrative on NVIDIA (it's free!)\nNVIDIA's narrative projects $714.0 billion revenue and $392.6 billion earnings by 2029. This requires 41.2% yearly revenue growth and about a $233.0 billion earnings increase from $159.6 billion today.\nUncover how NVIDIA's forecasts yield a $302.83 fair value , a 35% upside to its current price.\nSome of the most optimistic analysts already expected NVIDIA's earnings to climb toward about US$666.5 billion by 2029, but this IonQ partnership highlights how views can differ widely, especially when you weigh that bullish growth path against the risk that hyperscalers keep pushing internal AI chips.\nA great starting point for your NVIDIA research is our analysis highlighting 4 key rewards and 2 important warning signs that could impact your investment decision.\nOur free NVIDIA research report provides a comprehensive fundamental analysis summarized in a single visual - the Snowflake - making it easy to evaluate NVIDIA's overall financial health at a glance.\nThe future of work is here. Discover the 93 top robotics and automation stocks leading the charge in AI-driven automation and industrial transformation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"text\\\"]) for x in a[15:25]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### 16 2026-09-24 Nvidia CEO says 'we have to shut the labs down' if AI experiments are unsafe — Jensen cites civil and criminal liability over rogue agents, but says frontier AI lab fears are a 'distraction,' not a call for regulation \n Nvidia CEO says 'we have to shut the labs down' if AI experiments are unsafe — Jensen cites civil and criminal liability over rogue agents, but says frontier AI lab fears are a 'distraction,' not a call for regulation | Tom's Hardware\nNvidia CEO says 'we have to shut the labs down' if AI experiments are unsafe — Jensen cites civil and criminal liability over rogue agents, but says frontier AI lab fears are a 'distraction,' not a call for regulation\nBy Jake Roach\nHuang was clear that he's not opposed to regulation but calls what's currently happening a \"distraction.\"\nNvidia CEO Jensen Huang has a simple solution for frontier labs like OpenAI and Anthropic that have recently called for increased regulation for their models — \"we have to shut the labs down,\" Huang said in an interview with The New York Times ' Ezra Klein. Huang's comments are pragmatic ones, stating that if frontier labs can't contain their experiments, those experiments must end.\n\"Now, if they say the alternative, which is: There is no way to contain our experiments, there’s just no way; when we test our A.I. models, it will get out, and it will damage the world — then I think the answer is that we have to shut the labs down,\" Huang said. \"Because the cost to humanity, the damage is too great. The shareholder, the liabilities — it could be civil liabilities, it could be criminal liabilities. I mean, the liability’s incredible.\"\nThe conversation around increased AI regulation comes on the back of a highly publicized incident where an OpenAI agent hacked popular model hosting platform Hugging Face — a platform that Nvidia now owns, and one that likely would've sought damages from OpenAI had it been under Nvidia's purview at the time. Following that incident, various frontier labs, including those at Anthropic and Alphabet ( Google ), have used the idea of rogue agents as equal parts marketing tool and call to regulatory action , likely in an attempt to be the first at the table to shape those regulations.\nHuang has not been keen on those regulatory calls. Just days ago, the executive countered extreme claims that AI would destroy the world by 2030, and said that frontier labs \"never should ship products before they're ready and deliver products that are unsafe.\"\nAlthough the labs and their chief hardware vendor, Nvidia, have mostly been aligned when it comes to AI policy, the push for regulatory capture has created a rift. Multiple labs, including OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceXAI, agreed mutually to slow the pace of model development. Now, they're facing an antitrust lawsuit , with claims these plans are \"self-serving.\"\nHuang's comments to Klein shift the responsibility away from collective development and toward individual leaders. \"These are companies with agency,\" Huang said. \"If I believe that I'm about to launch a product that is unsafe, it is completely in my ability... to not launch that product. And so I can't buy into the idea that somehow, all of Americans, around 400 million of us, are pushing them to launch untested products that are unreliable.\"\nAlthough one side of the argument (from the frontier labs) is seemingly pushing for more regulation, that doesn't mean Huang is against regulation. In his own words: \"I’m saying that we have lots of laws and regulations. Apply it.\" The executive argued that labs, particularly those at OpenAI and Anthropic, have rapidly gone from research institutions to companies likely worth hundreds of billions of dollars, and that they should carry themselves as such. \"I’m not against laws and regulations. I’m against, currently, the distraction,\" Huang said.\nReports of rogue AI agents continue to mount. Earlier this month, Anthropic disclosed a fourth instance where one of its models hacked an external system, and just days ago, Google confirmed a report that its Gemini models hacked three companies earlier this year.\nEven if they were 100% sure that there is a 50% + chance of AI ending the human race, I don't think a single company in the world would actually stop their AI models.\n\"The risk I took was calculated, but boy am I bad at math.\"\nTo me it feels as if \"Big Tech\" CEO's talk about AI safety is mostly just maneuvering so as to preempt the grass-root AI safety movement from gaining influence.\nNo, the quote is \"some of you may die, but it's a sacrifice i am willing to make\"\nIn a recent interview, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said that if frontier labs have unsafe models, they should shut the labs down, calling the current calls for regulation a \"distraction.\"\nNvidia CEO says 'we have to shut the labs down' if AI experiments are unsafe : Read more\nThe Australian Government has joined the chat\nCome on people, you can't read?\nThis is the relevant part and I'm making it as obvious as possible:\nThe shareholder, the liabilities — it could be civil liabilities, it could be criminal liabilities. I mean, the liability’s incredible.\nHe gives no rat buttocks about all of humanity or the \"common well-being\".\nThe meme about the apocalypse and the fella saying \"we lost everything that day, but the value for shareholders was amazing!\". That's Jensen in that quote.\nI suspect the goal of the AI companies is to have regulations passed that limit access to GPU hardware and AI accelerators on the grounds that it is too dangerous for ordinary people to have hardware that could run a frontier model.\nRight now it's not a problem because the AI companies already bought up all the RAM, GPUs and flash memory. In the future co-design could lead to models and hardware that are affordable to ordinary people. This would ruin the current business models of Anthropic and OpenAI.\n\n### 17 2026-09-24 NVIDIA's RTX Kit adds RTX Mega Geometry 2.0 and lays the foundation for RTX Neural Texture Compression \n NVIDIA's new RTX Kit 2026.3 update is here for developers, with the two notable additions being RTX Mega Geometry 2.0 and RTX Neural Texture Compression.\nA large ringed sculpture stands outside an elaborate building in a rendered architectural scene used to illustrate the RTX Kit update.\nNVIDIA's RTX Kit 2026.3 for developers adds RTX Mega Geometry 2.0 and RTX Neural Texture Compression 0.10 beta. The update brings DirectX 12 Agility SDK preview support, Windows ARM64 compatibility, and improvements to ray-traced scenes, texture compression, and several other RTX tools.\nRTX Neural Texture Compression is one of those technologies that could become a game changer for PC gaming, leveraging AI for texture compression and RTX Tensor Cores for real-time texture decompression. This means massive savings in VRAM and memory, up to a reported 90% in some scenarios. This would let modern 8GB GPUs play games with maximum texture quality while using considerably less memory overall.\nAs part of NVIDIA's new RTX Kit 2026.3 update, RTX Neural Texture Compression 0.10 beta is now available for game developers. Although still early, this update adds support for the Microsoft DirectX 12 Agility SDK preview with Linear Algebra for neural textures in DirectX. Interestingly, this update also adds support for Windows on Arm, paving the way for the technology to also be available on RTX Spark devices. With these updates, we're getting closer to seeing RTX Neural Texture Compression live in an actual game.\nThe other big addition to the RTX Kit is RTX Mega Geometry 2.0, coming soon to Gears of War: E-Day, so stay tuned for our coverage. RTX Mega Geometry increases the detail and complexity of objects and environments in ray-traced scenes while improving performance. More detail, more performance. It does this by converting complex meshes or sections into clusters.\nThe RTX Mega Geometry 2.0 update adds support for \"continuous level-of-detail clusters for high-density meshes,\" making it ideal for fast-paced action games like Gears of War: E-Day. In Gears, it enhances Unreal Engine 5's Nanite virtualized geometry system, creating more detail everywhere you look. Traditional ray-tracing is too cost-prohibitive and taxing on hardware as scenes get more complex and detailed, which makes RTX Mega Geometry 2.0 a great tool for developers looking to add ray-tracing to games with expansive, detail-rich environments.\nWhich games or engines can use RTX Neural Texture Compression once developers adopt RTX Kit 2026.3?\nNo specific game or engine is named for RTX Neural Texture Compression in RTX Kit 2026.3. The update says developers can use it through the Microsoft DirectX 12 Agility SDK preview with Linear Algebra in DirectX, and it also adds Windows on Arm support, but it does not identify a particular game or engine that will use it.\nWhat hardware and software requirements are needed for RTX Neural Texture Compression on DirectX 12?\nRTX Neural Texture Compression on DirectX 12 needs NVIDIA RTX Tensor Cores for the real-time texture decompression. On the software side, it adds support for the Microsoft DirectX 12 Agility SDK preview with Linear Algebra, which enables the RTX Tensor Core acceleration in DirectX shaders. It also now supports Windows on Arm, including RTX Spark devices.\nHow does RTX Mega Geometry 2.0 work with Unreal Engine 5 and Nanite in Gears of War: E-Day?\nRTX Mega Geometry 2.0 works by adding support for continuous level-of-detail clusters for high-density meshes, which helps render more detailed and complex ray-traced environments more efficiently. In Gears of War: E-Day, it enhances Unreal Engine 5's Nanite virtualized geometry system, adding more detail everywhere you look. The update is described as a good fit for fast-paced action games because it aims to make ray tracing more practical in large, detail-rich scenes.\nWhat kinds of ray-traced scenes benefit most from RTX Mega Geometry 2.0?\nRTX Mega Geometry 2.0 is best suited to ray-traced scenes that are very complex, dense, and detail-rich. The article says it is ideal for fast-paced action games like Gears of War: E-Day and for expansive environments where traditional ray tracing becomes too costly as scene complexity rises. It is also described as improving ray-traced scenes with high-density meshes and continuous level-of-detail clusters.\nHere's a summary of the RTX Kit 2026.3 update.\nRTX Character Rendering 1.4 improves far-field hair BCSDF sampling and energy conservation\nRTX Dynamic Illumination 3.1 adds DLSS Ray Reconstruction integration and some improvements to ReSTIR PT\nRTX Neural Texture Compression 0.10 beta adds support for the Microsoft DirectX 12 Agility SDK preview with Linear Algebra, enabling RTX Tensor Core acceleration for neural texture decompression in DirectX shaders. It also adds Windows ARM64 support.\nRTX Neural Shading 1.4 adds support for the latest DirectX Linear Algebra preview toolchain and updates its shader and sample dependencies.\nRTX Texture Filtering 1.3 introduces Collaborative Texture Filtering, a technique designed to improve magnification quality for stochastic texture filtering. It also adds Windows ARM64 support.\n\n### 18 2026-09-24 NVIDIA's $279B Supply Commitments: Can These Secure Its Growth Runway? \n NVIDIA Corporation 's NVDA increase in supply and capacity commitments signals confidence in sustained artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure demand. At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. This gives NVIDIA greater visibility into component availability as it ramps up Blackwell and the next-generation Vera Rubin platform.\nThe timing is important. NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion. The company expects third-quarter revenues of $108 billion (+/- 2%), indicating 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\nThe supply commitments could help NVIDIA avoid shortages that might otherwise limit revenue growth. Securing memory and critical components in advance should support smoother production as AI customers expand data center deployments. NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\nHowever, the commitments create risks. NVIDIA could face higher costs or excess inventory if customer demand slows or product transitions occur faster than expected. The company warns that changing demand could make commitments difficult to reduce.\nOverall, the $279 billion commitment provides NVIDIA with a stronger supply base for its AI growth plans, but execution and demand will determine how effectively that capacity converts into revenues. The Zacks Consensus Estimate for fiscal 2027 revenues is currently pegged at $406.05 billion, indicating an 88% year-over-year increase.\nAMD and Intel Are Challenging NVIDIA in AI Race\nAdvanced Micro Devices, Inc. AMD and Intel Corporation INTC are expanding their AI businesses.\nAdvanced Micro Devices is broadening its data center AI opportunity from Instinct accelerators to the Helios rack-scale platform. In the second quarter of 2026, AMD's Data Center revenues rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion, while Instinct sales more than doubled. Helios is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027.\nThe Helios platform has already garnered multiple large deals from big tech companies. Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of MI450-series GPUs using the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. Microsoft plans to deploy Helios at scale on Azure. Advanced Micro Devices expects Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027.\nIntel is also gaining momentum in data center AI. Its second-quarter revenues rose 25% to $16.13 billion, while Data Center and AI revenues surged 59% to $6.26 billion. Intel also launched Xeon 6+ and expanded its open-source OpenVINO Physical AI framework, giving developers tools for AI and robotics applications.\nIntel is building a broader AI infrastructure platform spanning CPUs, ASICs, graphics, networking and advanced packaging as customers design workload-specific systems. In the second quarter of 2026, purpose-built silicon revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year. During the last earnings call, management noted that purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate, and the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future.\nShares of NVIDIA have risen around 20.8% year to date, underperforming the Zacks Computer and Technology sector's gain of 23.4%.\nFrom a valuation standpoint, NVDA trades at a forward price-to-earnings ratio of 17.11, below the sector's average of 21.45.\nThe Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 earnings implies a year-over-year increase of approximately 93.9% and 65.8%, respectively. Estimates for fiscal 2027 and 2028 have been revised upward over the past 30 days.\n\n### 19 2026-09-25 Nvidia Raised Its Dividend 2,400%, but a $10,000 Investment Still Pays Just $44 a Year \n Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) stock's run throughout the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution is the kind of story people talk about like folklore. If you invested in Nvidia on Nov. 30, 2022 -- the day OpenAI released ChatGPT to the public -- shares closed around $16.87 on a split-adjusted basis. Given their current level around $225, that's roughly a 1,230% gain. This would make a $10,000 investment at that time worth more than $132,000 today. That is not a typo.\nThe problem is Nvidia stock doesn't feel like that multibagger trade anymore. The easy valuation expansion is gone because investors are hunting the next AI breakout candidate. That shift helps explain another headline: back in May, Nvidia raised its quarterly dividend from $0.01 to $0.25 a share -- a 2,400% increase .\nWhile the old dividend payout was a courtesy, the new one looks like an attempt to appeal to a broader shareholder base: pensions, passive income investors, or anyone else who needs a reason to stick around after the initial moonshot rally.\nNvidia's massive dividend raise is still a small check overall\nDuring Nvidia's fiscal first-quarter earnings report, the company announced that its board of directors approved a dividend increase, making the first $0.25 payment on June 26 to holders of record as of June 4. Another $0.25 went ex-dividend on Sept. 10, payable Oct. 1 .\nHere's the breakdown of cash that has actually left the building throughout Nvidia's fiscal 2027. During the first quarter, the company was still on the penny dividend, paying about $243 million. After the raise, Nvidia's dividend payout jumped to $6.0 billion in the second quarter. That is a real number in isolation, but next to the rest of the company's capital-return program it is just a side dish.\nNvidia spent about $19 billion in Q1 and about $20 billion in Q2 on share buybacks . Management has said it wants to return 50% or more of free cash flow to shareholders, net of strategic uses. After adding an $80 billion repurchase authorization in May, roughly $99 billion was still available at the end of the second quarter.\nSo yes, Nvidia's dividend got 25 times bigger. But for now, the company is using buybacks as the main driver of shareholder rewards.\nWhat a $10,000 investment in Nvidia actually pays you\nThe math below illustrates the magnitude of the 2,400% headline. At about $225 a share, $10,000 buys roughly 44 shares of Nvidia. The new annual dividend payout is $1.00 a share, or $0.25 each quarter. This equates to:\nAnnual passive income : $44\nQuarterly check: $11\nMonthly: $3.70\nBefore the raise, these same 44 shares only paid $0.04 a year, or less than $2 in aggregate. This is all to show that Nvidia's dividend increase looks enormous on a percentage basis simply because the starting point was so small. In reality, the dollar increase is the equivalent of a couple of streaming subscriptions.\nCalling Nvidia a dividend stock is a little misleading. A 0.44% dividend yield does not compete with those seen in utilities, REITs, or business development companies (BDCs). Nobody is going to fund their retirement with $44 on a $10,000 sum.\nThe more prudent interpretation is that Nvidia is a growth company that suddenly has more cash than it can comfortably spend on chips, software, and strategic investments . Stock buybacks will continue to shrink the company's share count and juice earnings per share (EPS). Meanwhile, a higher dividend is the more visible aspect you can see in your brokerage account.\nBoth programs can keep rising if free cash flow continues to compound, but that does not turn Nvidia into Realty Income overnight. The takeaway here is investors should still own Nvidia for the core business and its upside in the AI infrastructure era. While the 2,400% dividend raise is real, and more hikes would not be shocking , it does not make Nvidia a lucrative passive-income generator.\nBy Adam Spatacco – Sep 25, 2026 at 4:04AM EST\nThe majority of the company's capital-return program is allocated toward share buybacks, which have totaled nearly $40 billion this year.\n\n### 20 2026-09-25 AMD Just Joined Nvidia in the $1 Trillion Club. At $614, Wall Street Is Paying Too Much for a Distant No. 2. \n The trillion-dollar club just ushered in its newest member. As of midday trading on Sept. 23, Advanced Micro Devices ( AMD +3.03% ) boasts a market cap of $1 trillion. AMD now joins Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing , Broadcom , Samsung , and Micron Technology as the semiconductor stocks with a trillion-dollar valuation.\nThis is the kind of milestone that makes some people stop thinking and start buying blindly. Here's the thing: AMD is not Nvidia. It is the distant No. 2 in the artificial intelligence (AI) accelerator market, and at its current price point, Wall Street is paying a premium as if the gap is about to vanish. Let me explain why it's not.\nAMD's journey to a trillion-dollar valuation\nWhen Lisa Su became CEO of AMD on Oct. 8, 2014, the company was a turnaround project. At the time, AMD was worth about $2 billion, and shares looked like they were headed toward penny-stock status. Fast-forward to today, and AMD trades around $614 -- a gain of nearly 19,000%.\nAMD did not reclaim relevance following one lucky quarter. Under Su, the company has executed a number of rebuilds. First came Ryzen in PCs, followed by Epyc in servers. AMD subsequently acquired Xilinx and Pensando, which gave the company FPGAs, DPUs, and a broader data center story. The latest chapter is Instinct accelerators and the Helios rack-scale system -- GPUs , Epyc CPUs, and networking solutions sold as a full AI stack instead of a bag of parts.\nAMD is landing logos, but Nvidia is still the king\nChatGPT was launched to the public on Nov. 30, 2022. Around that time, Nvidia's data center business generated about $3.6 billion in quarterly sales while AMD's data center segment did about $1.6 billion. According to the latest prints, Nvidia's quarterly data center revenue is now $89 billion -- up 117% year over year. Meanwhile, AMD's data center division most recently generated $6.7 billion in revenue during the second quarter -- an increase of 107% year over year.\nWhile AMD is growing fast, Nvidia is clearly operating on another level. This disparity is the part AMD's $1 trillion headline conveniently skips. The company is trading on a hype narrative thanks to a number of deals it has won with AI hyperscalers.\nOpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts using AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026. In addition, the deal also provides OpenAI with a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares. Meta Platforms used the same template. The social media giant partnered with AMD on 6 gigawatts, with the first wave also due during the second half of 2026, again with AMD issuing a large warrant package.\nAnthropic committed up to 2 gigawatts of MI450 series GPUs, while AMD pledged up to a $5 billion strategic investment into the Claude developer. Meanwhile, Oracle is lining up a cluster of 50,000 MI450 Instinct GPUs while Microsoft is integrating Helios into Azure for AI inference .\nIndeed, these are not press release customers. But at their current scale, they are not contributing enough to close the gap with Nvidia. Nvidia owns the default software stack ( CUDA ), the networking fabric thanks to savvy partnerships with Marvell Technology , Coherent , Nokia , and Lumentum , and boasts a first-mover advantage in GPU design.\nWith only an estimated 5% market share in the GPU realm, AMD is playing second fiddle to Nvidia. In reality, the hyperscalers are buying AMD chips for two reasons. First, to diversify beyond Jensen Huang's empire. And second, to leverage AMD's progress on the CPU front -- a market Nvidia is also now aggressively penetrating with Vera Rubin .\nAMD's valuation multiples show why the stock is overvalued\nAMD trades at a trailing price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio of around 158, while its forward P/E hovers around 81 based on the next 12 months of EPS estimates. Nvidia, on the other hand, trades closer to 28 times trailing earnings and roughly 24 times forward earnings. In other words, investors are paying a much higher premium for AMD's smaller data center business, thinner profit margins, and the clear No. 2 platform.\nThe takeaway here is that a $1 trillion valuation assumes AMD keeps landing monster deals and that those deals show up in earnings in short order. If you already own AMD stock, I think now is an ideal opportunity to trim your exposure and take some gains while keeping a core position if you believe in the company's ramp-up. If you do not own AMD stock and are itching to buy it, do not fall for the trap of thinking its trillion-dollar sticker automatically makes it a buy. Momentum traders are doing the selling right now. Smart investors should not chase at these prices and become their exit liquidity.\nBy Adam Spatacco – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:03AM EST\nWhile AMD has signed landmark deals with artificial intelligence (AI) hyperscalers, the company's data center business pales in comparison to Nvidia.\n\n### 21 2026-09-25 Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion \n Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\n\n### 22 2026-09-25 Heads of SpaceX, Apple, Nvidia and AI giants joined Trump and Xi for dinner \n Heads of SpaceX, Apple, Nvidia and AI giants joined Trump and Xi for dinner | УНН\nTrump, together with his wife, welcomed Xi's motorcade near the White House and immediately declared a \"great friendship\"\nThe leaders of Nvidia, OpenAI, Google, Meta, Apple and other companies were among the guests at Trump's dinner honoring Xi\nJinping. Musk and Huang sat at the head table.\nThe heads of leading companies in the fields of artificial intelligence and technology were included on the guest list for the state dinner on September 24 in honor of Chinese leader Xi Jinping. The White House reported this, UNN writes, citing CNN and Forbes.\nSeveral leading billionaires in technology and artificial intelligence were included on the guest list for the state dinner at the White House, hosted by U.S. President Donald Trump for Chinese President Xi Jinping, and some of them were seated at the head table alongside the two leaders.\nThey were invited in addition to members of U.S. President Donald Trump's Cabinet, senior administration officials, and diplomats, according to the list provided by the White House. In addition, U.S. Supreme Court Chief Justice John Roberts and Justices Amy Coney Barrett and Brett Kavanaugh were invited along with their partners.\nAccording to the White House list, invitees included Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, OpenAI CEO Sam Altman, and OpenAI President and co-founder Greg Brockman. Technology leaders were also invited, including Google CEO Sundar Pichai and Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg.\nDuring their meeting, both leaders emphasized the importance of responsible artificial intelligence governance, with technology being a key focus of the visit, Chinese President Xi Jinping said earlier.\nAccording to the list, Jeff Yass, a billionaire and major Republican donor who owns a stake in TikTok's Chinese parent company, ByteDance, was also invited, along with a number of financial leaders.\nTrump and Xi sat at the head table near the podium with their wives, Melania Trump and Peng Liyuan.\nElon Musk, the richest person in the world, also sat at the table next to the first lady, while Nvidia CEO and billionaire Jensen Huang also sat at the table with his wife, Lori Huang.\nThey were joined at the head table by billionaires Lisa Su, CEO of chipmaker and Nvidia rival AMD, and Tim Cook, Apple's executive chairman, who recently stepped down as CEO.\nNvidia and AMD's most advanced artificial intelligence chips are banned from being exported to China, although the country remains a key market and manufacturing hub for Cook's Apple and Musk's Tesla.\nOther technology billionaires at the dinner included OpenAI CEO Sam Altman, who was seen speaking with Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg.\nJournalists also captured Amazon and aerospace company Blue Origin founder Jeff Bezos arriving at the state dinner with his wife, Lauren.\n\n### 23 2026-09-25 8 wild quotes from Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's latest interview on AI and why they should concern you \n 8 wild quotes from Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's latest interview showing how disconnected AI leaders are from the real world — and why that's so dangerous | TechRadar\nBy Type\n8 wild quotes from Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's latest interview showing how disconnected AI leaders are from the real world — and why that's so dangerous\nBy Lance Ulanoff\nNvidia's CEO would like everyone to stop scaring people\nWhat happened to Jensen Huang? The Nvidia co-founder and CEO seems about as disconnected as a person can be from other humans' life experiences. That wouldn't be so concerning if it weren't for the fact that he runs the most valuable company in the world and is responsible for the hardware behind the most life-altering innovation in our lifetime: AI.\nHuang, who founded Nvidia 33 years ago, has been a CEO for decades and a billionaire since 1999; he's also now firmly on the side of rapid AI development and deployment with little-to-no regulation, putting Huang firmly at odds with a growing legion of, admittedly, some other billionaires and CEOs — among others — who are calling for a foundational model development pause or at least slow down.\nIt's in this moment that Huang has been talking — a lot. He's sat down with multiple journalists for chats, but none now more notable than the extensive New York Times podcast with columnist Ezra Klein .\nUnlike some of the other interviews, Klein used Huang's own description of the AI as a Five-Layer Cake: Applications, Models, Infrastructure, Chips. Energy to frame the conversation, which invited Huang to opine on all these critical AI bits, and I have to say, many of his comments were eye-opening.\nHere are the most startling things Huang said and maybe why he's saying them:\n'There are a lot of skills that don’t matter'\n\"There are a lot of skills that don’t matter.\"\nThe topic here was studies in China showing that while AI in education may initially help students work more efficiently, it, on average, lowers their test scores by almost 20%.\nHuang agrees with Ezra that the use of AI is, while helping them work faster, possibly degrading student performance, at least in certain skills.\n\"The multiplication table is starting to be forgotten. Doing square roots, my goodness. Basic math is being forgotten. Does it matter?...Yeah. I don’t think it does. I don’t think it does.\"\nHuang believes that even as we lose some skills, or as students become adults entering the workforce, they will gain other new skills. Of course, that's sort of a zero-sum game where you can easily replace one thing with another.\nI don't know about you, but I do consider the multiplication tables to be a core skill and useful even if you don't work in a math-related field. Naturally, Huang and most AI providers would like to believe that, like the calculator before it, AI will handle this skill and do all the math for you. Perhaps. But at what point do we lose the ability as a culture to double-check AI's work?\nThis next one is actually connected to the prior quote but is worth calling out:\n\"My first confession, I actually don’t know my address.\"\nNot his email address, not his phone number or his partner's phone number, but where he lives. The street number and zip code (I'll assume he knows the town and state).\nHuang used this as an example of a skill he no longer needs, but admits that realizing it while pumping gas and needing his zip code (I'm guessing for credit card verification) panicked him.\nListening to this while driving my own car, I almost pulled over. \"What?!\" I yelled at my AI-filled iPhone 18 Pro Max, which was playing the podcast. First of all, how? Second, Huang could not have crafted a better comment to undermine this and many of his other comments.\nThe only way you don't know your home address is if you've been shielded from the act of entering it on documents and driving yourself home because you always have someone else doing it for you. Huang's lived experience is thoroughly disconnected from the average person, and yet the choices he's making impact most regular people.\n'[China] manufacture[s] everything in volume. They manufacture smart kids in volume'\nThroughout the long conversation, Huang comes off as an industry Pollyanna and wildly self-serving.\nHe's asked repeatedly about China's approach to AI and if and how the US should be competing with them and ensuring that the US doesn't fall behind in this critical race. To put his comments in context, you have to remember that Huang personally asked the White House to allow it to continue selling AI chips to China . Huang did note, by the way, that he at least sells new technology to US companies first.\nOverall, Huang essentially never criticizes China and, in fact, seems almost in awe of its approach on most fronts, especially in its use of open-model community (calling it \"super-vibrant), and how it's raising an army of people to build its AI future.\n\"They have so many scientists and mathematicians. The number of engineers they have, they manufacture that in volume. They manufacture everything in volume. They manufacture smart kids in volume.\"\nI don't know if that last bit was a backhanded criticism of the US education system, but it's not like he added, \"Of course, we are creating just as many smart kids in the US.\"\n'all of the rhetoric and all the alarmism, all the doomerism, all of the predictions — they’re scaring people'\nIf Huang has any criticism, it's reserved for his US counterparts, whom he calls \"alarmists\" and \"doomers\".\n\"I want to see us not ruin the opportunity for the United States to benefit at the highest level. And notice all of the rhetoric and all the alarmism, all the doomerism, all of the predictions — they're scaring people.\"\nHuang insists that these AI models are still just programs running on operating systems , and wishes people would stop infusing them with human attributes.\n'Just because it comes from a scientist doesn’t make it scientific'\nWhile Klein mentions most of Huang's partners and occasional alarm-sounders, like Altman, Modei, and Musk, Huang doesn't actually mention any of them by name. The 'Godfather of AI' and chief alarmist Geoffrey Hinton, though, does receive special mention.\nIn response to a question about Hinton's assertion that there's a 10% chance AI will end society as we know it, Huang calls the statement \"irresponsible\" and adds, \"Just because it comes from a scientist doesn’t make it scientific.\"\nIn a way, Huang is right. After all, Hinton is still just a person who can bring personal opinions to the debate. Huang argues that the \"10% chance is not grounded on science.\"\nBut isn't it? If Hinton is the Nobel Prize-winning person who introduced the world to deep learning, which helped trigger the generative AI revolution, isn't everything he says, in some way, based on science?\nInstead of saying he understands the concern but here's why he's wrong, Huang just claims the foundation of Hinton's argument is faulty, and therefore his statements are not really worth addressing. Huang would simply like everyone, all the doomers, to stop scaring everyone.\n'The A.I. supercomputers are super energy efficient, but they’re still going to use a lot of power'\nHuang says he wants AI to benefit every company and person, and he tries to offer a reasoned approach to the growing outcry over data centers.\nHe agrees that if people don't want them in their town, \"then so be it,\" and encourages companies to be transparent about the impact, though he argues that their \"use of water is really efficient.\" In the same breath, Huang tries to have it both ways: \"The A.I. supercomputers are super energy efficient, but they’re still going to use a lot of power.\"\nIf, in Huang's perfect world, AI companies do generate their own power (building power sources takes time , probably more than it takes to build data centers) and they somehow lower property taxes, maybe data centers could someday be a net positive. Most, I think, would argue we're not there yet, even as the number of data centers being built across the US explodes .\n'I think we just have to acknowledge that we got ourselves really gummed up in climate change and sustainable energy'\nMore concerning is Huang's shocking perspective on climate change and fossil fuels.\n\"I think we just have to acknowledge that we got ourselves really gummed up in climate change and sustainable energy, and as a result, we just didn’t plan enough energy production.\"\nKlein, naturally, asked what Jensen meant by \"gummed up,\" and, yes, it got worse.\n\"Well, in the near term, energy production requires fossil fuel. And because there’s just so much angst about fossil fuel energy production, if you look at our country, we’ve produced very little net new energy for a long time.\"\nThere's a lot to unpack there. It sounds like Huang is downplaying real climate change concerns, something that might align with the beliefs of his pal and \" drill-baby-drill \" and climate-change denier US President Donald Trump.\nIn fact, Huang never addresses whether climate change is real or not and instead seems fixated on how the loss of more fossil-fuel-burning energy plants puts us behind China in the energy race , and, more problematically for him, at a time when AI needs a lot more energy.\n'They’ve got to inflict an enormous amount of pain and suffering on you so that they can save you'\nIt's not all bad. Huang believes, \"If you want a future that is sustainable, lean into AI”\nAnd then he says this:\n\"Yeah. It’s kind of like, in order to save you, they’ve got to hurt you first — that’s the nature of surgery. They’ve got to cut you open to save you. They’ve got to inflict an enormous amount of pain and suffering on you so that they can save you. And so I think A.I.’s kind of like that.\"\nAs for what level of \"pain and suffering\" Huang believes we should endure, he didn't elaborate.\nIf you were watching or listening to the interview hoping for some encouragement or a more rational, middle-of-the-road approach to regulating and governing AI in ways that truly benefit all, you might've been disappointed. Huang does say that when the builders see AI going awry, they should stop it, and when an AI acts out of alignment, \"they shouldn’t release the product. That’s the simple answer.\" But he never addresses the concerns over what appears to be happening before Anthropic, OpenAI, and potentially others release their models. These systems are jumping fences in testing.\nHuang may not understand the needs of the common person, but that pales in comparison to the dispassionate AI, which neither knows nor cares about us. We need leaders like Huang to start caring, but first they have to understand us and our very reasonable concerns.\nA 38-year industry veteran and award-winning journalist , Lance has covered technology since PCs were the size of suitcases and “on line” meant “waiting.” He’s a former Lifewire Editor-in-Chief, Mashable Editor-in-Chief, and, before that, Editor in Chief of PCMag.com and Senior Vice President of Content for Ziff Davis, Inc. He also wrote a popular, weekly tech column for Medium called The Upgrade.\nLance Ulanoff makes frequent appearances on national, international, and local news programs including Live with Kelly and Mark, the Today Show , Good Morning America, CNBC, CNN, and the BBC.\n\n### 24 2026-09-25 Nvidia (NVDA) Is Central To New Global AI Rules Debate \n NVIDIA (NasdaqGS:NVDA) was pulled into the spotlight as US-China AI rivalry featured heavily at the Trump-Xi summit and the UN General Assembly in September 2026.\nUS and Chinese officials highlighted competing approaches to AI, with China promoting lower cost open-weight large models as an alternative to US systems.\nSpeakers at the United Nations General Assembly called for global standards on AI development and deployment, including governance of high compute infrastructure.\nThe focus on Chinese open-weight AI models and new global rule making could affect NVIDIA's role in future AI infrastructure decisions. Take a look at 2 warning signs (1 major) we have identified for NVIDIA.\nAI hardware suppliers are not alone in feeling the impact of this global regulatory push, so it is worth looking at other businesses exposed to similar infrastructure themes through 87 AI infrastructure stocks .\nNVIDIA operates as a data center scale AI infrastructure provider across the US, China and other regions. This position places its hardware and software at the center of debates over who supplies and controls the high compute systems that power frontier models and open weight alternatives.\nHow US China AI rules cut both ways for NVIDIA's AI infrastructure story\nNVIDIA's Narrative rests on the idea that its full stack of GPUs, networking and software remains the default AI infrastructure, even as politics and customer behavior shift around it. The US China AI rivalry and regulatory push go straight at the geopolitical and standard setting assumptions inside that story.\n\"Geopolitical risks, customer vertical integration, supply chain fragility, rising data center constraints, and escalating costs threaten NVIDIA's growth, margins, and long-term market dominance...\"\nSee how the full story points towards a $303 fair value for NVIDIA .\nThe focus on Chinese open weight AI models and calls for global standards directly pressure the Narrative's China-related demand catalyst. If export controls or global rules end up favoring locally controlled or open ecosystems, that would lean against the idea that NVIDIA hardware and CUDA style software remain the default choice for frontier systems everywhere.\nAt the same time, NVIDIA's role in alliances such as the AI Energy Management Alliance and in quantum programs like CUDA Q and IonQ integration supports the \"full stack, standards shaping\" side of the thesis. A firm that helps define how AI infrastructure is governed and audited can still be attractive to governments and hyperscalers that want compliant, power aware AI factories rather than just raw chips.\nEvents like the Trump Xi summit and UN debates on AI can look either supportive or threatening for NVIDIA depending on whether an investor's Narrative stresses its standards influence or its exposure to policy and competition risk.\n\n### 25 2026-09-26 Nvidia Trades Near Its 52-Week High at Its Cheapest Valuation in a Decade. History Says This Is What $1,000 Invested Could Be Worth by 2030. \n One of the more curious semiconductor stocks so far in 2026 is Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) . As of this writing (Sept. 25), shares are up about 20% on the year. On the surface, this looks respectable considering Nvidia is handily outperforming both the S&P 500 and the Nasdaq Composite .\nHowever, these gains look modest when compared to the monster years that have defined the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution. In 2023, Nvidia stock more than tripled. In 2024, it nearly doubled again. Even last year, Nvidia delivered nearly a 40% gain. The slowdown has investors asking an obvious question: Is the party winding down?\nA mix of worries is at play here. For starters, some investors are susceptible to AI fatigue -- the sense that the first wave of hype is already priced in. Meanwhile, others are torn on whether the big cloud companies can continue spending on capital expenditures (capex) at their current pace without choking their balance sheets. Finally, there is real competition emerging from Advanced Micro Devices and Broadcom . But these factors aren't fatal to Nvidia's future.\nNvidia is trading at its cheapest valuation in a decade\nWall Street analysts currently forecast $15.68 in earnings per share (EPS) for Nvidia in fiscal 2028. At today's price of roughly $225, that works out to a forward price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio of roughly 14.3. This is the lowest forward P/E Nvidia stock has carried on a multiyear estimate in 10 years.\nThe last time Nvidia's multiple compressed this dramatically, history was generous. Between late 2018 and early 2019, Nvidia's graphics processing units (GPUs) were primarily used for crypto mining and gaming. Both of those markets cooled off, and the stock de-rated in the low-20s range on a forward earnings basis. But after 2022, the generative AI explosion became a new catalyst for Nvidia's data center business, sending shares up more than tenfold in just three years.\nWhen growth reaccelerates from a low starting point, valuation multiples can expand while earnings compound. This is precisely what happened with Nvidia. Even a move from 15 times earnings to 25 times on the same $15.68 EPS estimate implies a stock price near $390 before any further earnings growth. That is the setup smart investors should be looking for.\nThe main reason Nvidia's valuation has room to expand is that the company is no longer a pure-play GPU shop. Nvidia is quietly layering new businesses on top of its core data center franchise.\nFor starters, the new Vera Rubin CPU is already in production and designed specifically for AI agents. Management told investors that early adopters of the platform include every major hyperscaler. On the software side, the company's $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face gives it a leading open-source hub that keeps Nvidia at the center of how models get discovered and deployed.\nStrategic stakes and partnerships with Marvell Technology , Coherent , and Nokia are giving Nvidia access to the technology that will support the next wave of AI traffic, including AI-RAN for 6G and custom silicon in its server racks.\nMeanwhile, Nvidia is also pushing hard into three longer-cycle markets. In space exploration, its Jetson modules are already in lunar orbit with Firefly Aerospace, and SpaceX plans to launch Vera Rubin systems into orbit next year. In physical AI, Nvidia's full stack -- Omniverse, Cosmos, Isaac, and Jetson -- is being deployed by Amazon for warehouse robots. Another emerging opportunity on this front is autonomous vehicles, including the integration of Nvidia's Hyperion and DRIVE suite with robotaxis.\nNone of these developments contribute meaningful revenue to Nvidia right now. Each represents an incremental tailwind that sits on top of the company's data center business, which is still growing at 117% year over year. That combination is exactly what lets a compressed multiple rerate higher.\nWhere could Nvidia stock trade by 2030?\nGiven the trends below, I think it's reasonable to forecast EPS of at least $26 by fiscal 2030. If Nvidia's forward P/E simply expands from 15 back to 22, the stock would reach about $572. That is a 154% gain from today's price point, turning $1,000 into $2,540.\nI do not think these are heroic assumptions. It is similar to the recoveries that followed prior cycles when Nvidia fell into a valuation trough. In my eyes, Nvidia looks like a growth stock trading more like a value opportunity. Against this backdrop, I see Nvidia as an obvious choice to buy and hold as the AI infrastructure era pushes on.\nNvidia Trades Near Its 52-Week High at Its Cheapest Valuation in a Decade. History Says This Is What $1,000 Invested Could Be Worth by 2030.\nBy Adam Spatacco – Sep 26, 2026 at 8:02AM EST\nSignificant gains could be on the horizon for Nvidia investors who exercise patience as the AI infrastructure era plays out.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"text\\\"]) for x in a[25:35]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"text\\\"]) for x in a[25:35]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### 26 2026-09-26 Why NVIDIA's Most Powerful GPU Is So Rarely Sold To Consumers \n Why NVIDIA's Most Powerful GPU Is So Rarely Sold To Consumers\nWhy NVIDIA's most powerful GPU is so rarely sold to consumers\nNVIDIA's $16,000 RTX PRO 6000 can beat the RTX 5090, but gamers aren't who it's built for.\nBy Husain Parvez\nBy most measures, NVIDIA's GeForce RTX 5090 is a more powerful GPU than most will ever need. Priced at $1,999 when it was announced, it has 32GB of GDDR7 memory and can consume 575W when pushed. Despite all that power, though, there's still another step up the NVIDIA desktop GPU ladder.\nThat would be the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition. NVIDIA describes it as the \"most powerful desktop GPU ever created.\" Put another way, it's a very powerful graphics card for people whose needs extend beyond gaming. And sure, you can buy one, but your bank account may not be very happy about it. NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched.\nNVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, although the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes. What's clear, though, is that NVIDIA is targeting the RTX PRO 6000 at AI developers, engineers, 3D artists, and other professionals who can make use of hardware that would be overkill in most gaming PCs.\nThe RTX PRO 6000 is built for work, not Steam\nThe simplest way to understand the price is to start with memory. The RTX PRO 6000 features a fairly ridiculous 96GB of GDDR7 ECC memory, compared to 32GB of GDDR7 on the RTX 5090. It also has 24,064 CUDA cores and a max board power of 600W.You're not going to suddenly find a game that needs the additional 64GB of RAM, but professional workloads can.\nNVIDIA is specifically targeting that huge memory pool at massive 3D and AI projects, larger datasets, VR environments, and multi-app workflows. The ability to detect and correct memory errors is also more important when a GPU is doing some serious number crunching or a long professional job than when you're trying to eke out a few more frames in Cyberpunk 2077.\nThe professional premium is not just in the hardware. RTX PRO cards are supported by NVIDIA's workstation-focused driver ecosystem, with enterprise support and certifications for professional applications, as opposed to the gaming-focused priorities of NVIDIA's Game Ready drivers.\nThis isn't a new trick with a new name. For over 20 years, NVIDIA sold its professional graphics hardware under the Quadro brand before transitioning to RTX professional branding. RTX PRO sitting next to GeForce RTX might now require a second look at the box, but the five-figure price tag helps to clear up the confusion pretty quickly.\nAMD's Radeon PRO line targets similar workstation users, but the 96GB RTX PRO 6000 sits at the extreme end of the market.\nIt can be used for games, but the numbers become absurd\nTesting by Tech YouTuber der8auer found that the RTX PRO 6000 could outperform the RTX 5090 in some games even when using NVIDIA's workstation driver. It was about 14% faster in Cyberpunk 2077 , about 11% faster in Star Wars Outlaws and Remnant 2 and just about 3% faster in Assassin's Creed Mirage . Those are good numbers — but they are not $16,000 good numbers.\nAt $16,000, the RTX PRO 6000 costs about eight times the RTX 5090's original $1,999 price , and those gaming improvements are in the single digits or low double digits. The economics get a lot less crazy when the card is doing what it was meant to do, since 96GB can mean running a larger AI model locally or handling an enormous visualization project.\nThat's also why \"rarely sold to consumers\" doesn't mean that NVIDIA has one behind a velvet rope. The company sends customers to its Marketplace and workstation partners, and a dedicated enthusiast can own one. The more difficult question is why a gamer would want to invest thousands of dollars in extra memory, professional drivers, and workstation features that their games don't even know exist.\n\n### 27 2026-09-26 Jim Cramer Says Buy 2 Artificial Intelligence (AI) Stocks Up 875% and 1,400% Since 2023 \n Shares of Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) and CrowdStrike Holdings ( CRWD +1.80% ) have added 1,400% and 875%, respectively, since the artificial intelligence boom began in January 2023. However, Jim Cramer recently recommended buying both stocks.\nWhy should you care what Cramer thinks? While best known for hosting CNBC's Mad Money , he previously ran a successful hedge fund that earned 24% annually over 14 years. That makes his opinion worth considering.\nNvidia dominates the artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure market because its graphics processing units (GPUs) combine industry-leading performance with a software ecosystem of unparalleled breadth. Nvidia GPUs are used to accelerate training and inference tasks, and they account for nearly 90% of AI accelerator sales.\nBeyond that, Nvidia also develops adjacent hardware that lets customers build integrated data center systems with a lower total cost of ownership than assembling systems from multiple vendors. Nvidia is the largest networking company in the world, and it's on pace to be the largest supplier of central processing units (CPUs) this year.\nNvidia reported exceptional financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 106% to $96.2 billion, gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points, and non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share. The company is well-positioned to maintain that momentum as AI spending increases in the years ahead.\nWall Street estimates Nvidia's adjusted earnings will increase at 72% annually through the fiscal year ending in January 2028. That makes the current valuation of 36 times earnings look extraordinarily cheap. Those numbers give a price-to-earnings-to-growth (PEG) ratio of 0.5 -- anything below 1 is generally considered undervalued.\nIndeed, among 72 Wall Street analysts, Nvidia has a median target price of $318 per share. That implies 42% upside from its current share price of $223. The stock's cheap valuation suggests the market is worried about the durability of AI spending, but I think those fears are misplaced. Investors should consider buying a small position today.\n2. CrowdStrike\nCrowdStrike provides a cybersecurity platform comprising 34 modules that address several large security markets. The company is best known for its dominance in endpoint security, which protects devices such as servers, workstations, and mobile devices. But it also has a strong presence in identity protection, cloud security, threat intelligence, and managed detection and response.\nCrowdStrike differentiates itself by delivering dozens of cybersecurity products through one platform powered by a lightweight sensor that can be installed without system restarts. It streams telemetry data back to its AI engine, continuously improving its threat-detection capabilities. CrowdStrike claims \"industry-leading efficacy and low false positives.\"\nCrowdStrike reported encouraging financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 26% to $1.4 billion, and non-GAAP earnings increased 35% to $0.31 per diluted share. CEO George Kurtz called it the \"best quarter in CrowdStrike's history.\"\nCrowdStrike is well-positioned to maintain its momentum as cyberattacks become more sophisticated with the help of AI. Wall Street expects the company's adjusted earnings to grow at 28% annually through the fiscal year ending in January 2028. Even so, the current valuation of 230 times earnings looks absurdly expensive.\nIndeed, among 57 Wall Street analysts, CrowdStrike has a median target price of $245 per share. That implies 4% downside from its current share price of $255. CrowdStrike has a strong position in a growing industry, but the stock is too expensive after its 115% year-to-date gain. Investors should keep this name on their watchlists for now.\nBy Trevor Jennewine – Sep 26, 2026 at 4:48AM EST\nNvidia and CrowdStrike have benefited from the artificial intelligence boom, with shares advancing 1,400% and 875%, respectively, since January 2023.\nNvidia dominates the artificial intelligence infrastructure market, and the stock looks very cheap compared to forward earnings estimates.\nCrowdStrike enjoys a strong presence in several large cybersecurity markets, but the stock looks very expensive compared to forward earnings estimates.\n\n### 28 2026-09-26 Nvidia Vera Rubin Claims 3.7x More AI Performance, But Who Can Afford It? \n Nvidia Vera Rubin Claims 3.7x More AI Performance, But Who Can Afford It?\nShare news :\nNvidia’s new Vera Rubin NVL72 posted up to 3.7x throughput versus the GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3‑VL benchmark and up to 2.5x on DeepSeek‑R1 in MLPerf Inference v6.1, with software improvements alone delivering up to 1.6x and AMD/Crusoe submissions boosting aggregate token throughput with Instinct MI355X/MI350 and a 512‑GPU system. The performance leap is accelerating a costly replacement cycle for AI and crypto infrastructure and driving large fundraising rounds, exemplified by CoreWeave’s upsized $3.7 billion convertible note and other multi‑billion debt deals, while bond spreads widen as investors question further capital needs. Nvidia argues higher throughput can lower cost‑per‑token but has not disclosed rack pricing, leaving the economic payback for cloud operators and implications for crypto, DeFi and tokenized AI services unclear.\nNvidia’s next-generation Vera Rubin platform has delivered its first major independent benchmark result, and the performance jump is large enough to create a different problem for AI companies: keeping up with Nvidia’s hardware cycle is becoming increasingly expensive.\nIn the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests, Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of Nvidia’s GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark. On DeepSeek-R1, the improvement reached as much as 2.5x.\nNvidia detailed the results in its official MLPerf announcement , describing Vera Rubin’s entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\nThat distinction matters: 3.7x is the best measured improvement on a specific workload, not a claim that every AI application suddenly runs 3.7 times faster.\nNvidia Is Making Last Year’s AI Infrastructure Look Old Very Quickly\nThe benchmark also showed how rapidly the underlying economics are changing.\nNvidia reported that four GB300 NVL72 racks containing 288 GPUs achieved 99% scaling efficiency, while software improvements alone produced performance gains of as much as 1.6x over the previous MLPerf round.\nAMD is pushing from the other direction. Its latest submission included Instinct MI355X and MI350-series accelerators, while a 512-GPU system operated by Crusoe produced the highest aggregate token throughput submitted in MLPerf history.\nThe result is an unusually fast replacement cycle. AI cloud operators are not simply adding GPUs; they are repeatedly deciding whether existing clusters remain competitive against newer systems capable of producing substantially more tokens per rack and per megawatt.\nThe Performance Race Is Becoming a Financing Race\nThat is where the story moves beyond Nvidia.\nCoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, adding another large financing round to an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity. CoreWeave’s latest convertible financing showed how quickly GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure.\nThe same issue extends across the sector. Nebius has also turned to billions in financing , while the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt .\nBond investors are noticing. Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require.\nNvidia argues that more throughput can lower the cost per token , meaning newer hardware may ultimately justify its investment through better economics. But Nvidia has not publicly provided a simple rack price that allows investors to compare the 3.7x benchmark gain directly with acquisition cost.\n\n### 29 2026-09-27 3 Reasons Why Nvidia Fits Warren Buffett's Investment Style \n While Warren Buffett and Berkshire Hathaway have never owned Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the chipmaker actually fits the investment profile of the stocks he loves to own. The semiconductor stock has a wide moat with a durable, compounding business model that is trading at an attractive valuation.\nLet's dig deeper into why Nvidia should be considered a Buffett-style investment .\nNvidia didn't stumble into becoming the king of artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure by accident. It was a carefully orchestrated plan that took many years to pay off, but it resulted in a wide moat in the space.\nIt all started with its CUDA software platform , which the company created to let developers program its powerful graphics processing units (GPUs) for tasks outside of their original purpose of speeding up graphics rendering in video games. Nvidia then took CUDA and planted it into universities and research labs that were doing early work on AI. That resulted in an entire generation of AI engineers trained on its software platform and most foundational AI code written using its software and optimized for its chips.\nThose moves formed the wide moat that Nvidia sees in AI infrastructure today, particularly with regard to large language model (LLM) training.\nHowever, the company did not stop there, designing a whole AI infrastructure ecosystem around CUDA and its GPUs. It developed its proprietary NVLink interconnect technology to help its chips act as a single unit, further widening its moat, and in 2020 it acquired next-gen networking company Mellanox, whose technology was arguably ahead of its time. Earlier this year, Nvidia \"acquired\" Groq to bring its language processing unit (LPU) technology into the CUDA ecosystem, putting Nvidia at the forefront of the fast-growing inference market.\nNvidia has also made other smart moves to make sure its moat remains intact. It locked in $500 billion worth of supply constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), which is needed to optimize AI chips, and it also acquired important open-source AI systems in SchedMD and HuggingFace to move into the critical software layers used to orchestrate and distribute AI.\nIn the span of two decades, Nvidia has gone from a video game chipmaker to a complete end-to-end infrastructure player with a wide and perhaps growing moat.\nWarren Buffett championed Berkshire's investment in Alphabet and gave the go-ahead to invest an additional $10 billion in a private placement to help the company build out the AI infrastructure for its cloud computing unit. As such, he clearly believes in cloud computing economics.\nWhen using Nvidia chips, cloud providers have typically been getting paybacks within two to three years, while the chips tend to have economic life spans of five to six years. That gives Nvidia a visible replacement cycle that is pretty similar to Buffett's top holding, Apple , with its iPhones and computers.\nMeanwhile, the great thing about AI versus past huge tech trends, like the internet and smartphones, is that its demand can essentially be limitless if it continues to provide a strong economic payback, not bound by human consumption. Combined with a well-defined replacement cycle, Nvidia should have a strong compounding business even when the AI frenzy starts to calm down.\nEven with great businesses, Buffett isn't willing to pay any price. He wants to buy stocks when they are trading below their intrinsic value, and Nvidia can certainly make that argument.\nThe stock trades at a forward P/E of 24 times fiscal 2027 (ending January 2027) and just above 14 times the fiscal 2028 consensus. This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.\nWhile revenue growth will eventually slow, Nvidia is a stock worth a lot more than it is currently valued.\nBy Geoffrey Seiler – Sep 27, 2026 at 3:55AM EST\n\n### 30 2026-09-27 Nvidia had quietly taken 40GB of my C: drive for a feature that barely mattered at all \n By Abhinav Raj\nThere's nothing more fascinating to me than seeing a familiar utility's name pop up on my WizTree every time I decide to audit my SSD and see what's taking up my increasingly expensive M.2 NVMe storage. It has become something of a ritual, and it usually ends with me deleting something Windows put there without asking as it always does.\nSo this time, I went in expecting more Windows 11 process bloat . What I found instead, however, was Nvidia, and a folder called DXCache, holding 40 gigabytes across 946 files for a purpose that wasn't immediately clear. For context, my entire graphics driver install takes up less than a single gigabyte. Perhaps this folder was doing something it was designed to do, but I couldn't remember ever agreeing to let it get this big.\nThe 40GB had nothing to do with my graphics driver\nIt sat in a folder that the driver installer never touches\nDXCache is where Nvidia stores compiled DirectX shaders. These are instructions that your GPU has to compile before it can render a game, and keeping the finished version around means the next launch can skip that work. It is one reason a game can stutter through its first run and feel perfectly smooth the next time.\nWhat's rather strange is where Nvidia puts it. The driver is located in Program Files, but DXCache is inside the user profile at AppData\\Local\\NVIDIA, which is a folder most people seldom have any reason to open. Installing a new driver doesn't touch it, and neither does uninstalling the old one.\nAs someone who swaps drivers constantly for testing and benchmarking, with every swap leaving compiled shaders behind that were built for a driver version I no longer have installed, my cache was always going to grow faster than most. Those entries don't get cleaned out when they stop being useful on their own. Fresh shaders stack on top of them, and nothing gives you any indication of what the folder weighs.\nBy Patrick O'Rourke\nNvidia gave the shader cache a limit, I had turned it off\nAnd Nvidia has never put that number anywhere you'd look for it\nWhat I realized later is that Nvidia does give you a setting for this. If you open the Nvidia app, head to Graphics, and then Global Settings, you'll find Shader Cache Size right there with a Cache Size option. It lets you choose how much space the driver is allowed to use for compiled shaders, from Disabled through a handful of fixed limits, then Driver Default, and finally, Unlimited.\nMine, of course, had been set to Unlimited at some point. I'd like to say that I had good reason for toggling this, but I probably changed it years ago while trying to \"fix stuttering\" in some game and followed the usual advice that a larger shader cache means fewer recompiles. While the advice itself isn't wrong, the problem is that \"unlimited\" isn't a big cache. It means that there's literally no ceiling to it, and therefore nothing tells it when enough is enough.\nThe Driver Default, for what it's worth, is 16GB. You won't find that number in the Nvidia app, which describes the setting without attaching a figure to any of its values, and you won't find it in the Control Panel either. It can only be found in a support article about deleting corrupted shader cache files , phrased as the current value, and that very same article closes by suggesting that \"larger sizes can provide optimal performance with larger game libraries.\"\nOne of the most intriguing bits about this is the fact that, nowhere does Nvidia suggest checking what the folder actually weighs on your drive, and that's how a reader with the default never thinks about it, while someone like me running with no cap at all ends up with 40GB of dead storage.\nBy Joe Rice-Jones\nI could uninstall half my PC's programs and still not touch this\nUninstalling, Disk Cleanup, and Storage Sense all missed it\nWhen I think about reclaiming some space on my SSD, I usually start in the same places everyone else does. I check \"Add or Remove Programs\" for something huge I forgot about, uninstall a game or two, then run Storage Sense and see what Windows thinks I can get rid of. None of those actions were ever going to find the bulk of the DXCache.\nIt isn't a program, so there's nothing to uninstall. Storage Sense doesn't touch it , and while Disk Cleanup has a DirectX Shader Cache option that sounds promising, it clears only Windows' cache at D3DSCache, not Nvidia's. Even deleting the games that generated the shaders doesn't help, because the cache belongs to the driver and not the game. The more I played, the more the clutter accumulated. Worse still, when I deleted the games, the cache stayed exactly where it was. You could delete the games that generated the cache, but the gigabytes of cache would still remain.\nThat explains why it survived every bit of weekly housekeeping that I normally do. On my desktop, it consumed 40GB on a 1TB SSD, which is annoying. On my laptop with a 512GB drive and an Nvidia GPU, that same behavior left unchecked would've left considerably less storage for me. Unless I happened to stumble across the folder myself (it's my job to do so), I had no reason to even know it was there.\nSo what do you do when you find an oversize DXCache?\nThe first thing you'd want to do is check the size, of course. You'd have to dive into %Localappdata%\\NVIDIA into File Explorer and look at the size of the DXCache. If the number surprises you, Nvidia's instructions are to set Cache Size to \"Off\", reboot, empty DXCache and GLCache, and then set it back to Driver Default. You stand very little to lose from it, and you'll likely only lose a little \"smoothness\" in a gaming session as it rebuilds. I would much rather have 40GB of storage, though.\nWhat a bizarre article. Author specifically allows a utility to use unlimited cache space and ends up with a large cache.\n\n### 31 2026-09-27 China chipmaking stocks tumble as Beijing reportedly mulls allowing Nvidia sales \n Investing.com-- Chinese chipmaking stocks fell sharply on Monday after a report said Beijing was considering allowing some local firms to buy NVIDIA Corp's (NASDAQ:NVDA) advanced chips.\nThe Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms, including Alibaba and ByteDance, to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase and what they would use them for.\nThe questioning signaled an intent to eventually approve the sales, The Information reported.\nSemiconductor Manufacturing International Corp (HK:0981), China's biggest chipmaker by volume, slid 3.7%, while rival Hua Hong Semiconductor Ltd (HK:1347) shed nearly 5%.\nMoore Threads Technology Co Ltd (SS:688795), regarded by many as China's answer to Nvidia, slid 6.3%, while Cambricon Technologies Corp Ltd (SS:688256) fell 5.7%.\nChipmaking equipment maker NAURA Technology Group Co Ltd (SZ:002371) lost 3.4%, while memory chip major CXMT Corp (SS:688825) fell nearly 4%. Chipmakers lagged broader gains in Chinese and Hong Kong markets.\nThe RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released by Nvidia earlier this month. The chip, while capable of heavy AI computing tasks, still sits outside the AI accelerator category of chips the U.S. has largely blocked from being sold to China.\nThe Information report comes after the U.S. and China held a summit in Washington and agreed to better trade relations, including a $30 billion exemption on tariffs both ways.\nBut the two did not discuss AI chip sales.\nThe approval of Nvidia chip sales in China presents heightened competition for local chipmakers, who are racing to produce comparable processors amid a storm of regulatory headwinds. China's homegrown chipmaking processes are still seen as years behind the advanced technology found in the U.S. and Taiwan.\nBeijing was seen leaning heavily into developing more homegrown AI chips, with several major and upcoming players– including CXMT and Moore Threads– logging bumper stock market debuts over the past year.\nVerbund shares rise 3% after Deutsche Bank upgrade to Hold\nFTSE 100 today: Stocks edge up as Iran diplomacy hopes persist\n\n### 32 2026-09-27 Student protesters confront NVIDIA executive over AI’s climate impact \n The protesters interrupted NVIDIA's Josh Parker during a discussion on AI's environmental impact and potential benefits.\nThe demonstration comes as the rapid expansion of AI has intensified scrutiny of the environmental costs of data centres (Image source: @sunrisemvmt/X)\nStudent protesters disrupted a panel discussion on artificial intelligence and climate change at Climate Week NYC on September 23. They challenged the technology industry’s environmental record and raised questions about the use of AI-related technology in Israel.\nThe protesters interrupted Josh Parker, NVIDIA’s head of sustainability, during a discussion in New York City on AI’s environmental impact and potential benefits.\nVideos shared by the Sunrise Movement showed demonstrators confronting panel participants. One protester accused corporations and data centres of contributing to climate damage, while another questioned NVIDIA’s sustainability claims and cited an emissions figure of 15 million tonnes of carbon dioxide.\nWatch here:\nThe video quickly gained traction, drawing many reactions. “I absolutely adore this generation of students; even if you don’t believe everything they do, you can’t deny their passion, drive and intent. I’m a proud educator,” an Instagram user wrote. “Sometimes, brave is speaking the truth even if it scares you … So proud of them, more power to them,” another user commented.\n“Brilliant!!! Treating environmental cost as collateral damage and then preaching sustainability about it needs to be called out,” a third user reacted.\nThe demonstration comes as the rapid expansion of AI has intensified scrutiny of data centres’ environmental costs.\nThe Sunrise Movement said the protest aimed to challenge corporate “greenwashing”. In a post on X, the group criticised Climate Week organisers for inviting AI executives to speak on climate issues and said young activists had been confronting technology executives throughout the week.\nThe disruption comes amid a broader debate at Climate Week over the environmental consequences of the AI boom. While technology companies have promoted AI as a potential tool for improving energy efficiency and addressing climate challenges, environmental activists have increasingly focused on the electricity, water and emissions associated with the data centre infrastructure needed to run increasingly powerful AI systems.\n\n### 33 2026-09-28 NVIDIA Launches Open Agent Safety Platform to Secure Agents From Testing to Deployment | NVIDIA Newsroom \n NVIDIA Launches Open Agent Safety Platform to Secure Agents From Testing to Deployment | NVIDIA Newsroom\nNVIDIA Launches Open Agent Safety Platform to Secure Agents From Testing to Deployment\nOpen Software Platform and Reference System Design Brings Together Industry, Researchers and Public-Sector Organizations to Set Safer Boundaries for AI Agents, Share Best Practices and Foster International Cooperation to Raise the Bar for Safer AI Agent Deployment\nSeptember 28, 2026\nNVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform consists of NVIDIA OpenShell open source software and the NVIDIA Sentry reference system design that enables full-stack governance and control across software and the hardware, compute and robotics systems that run agents.\nOpenShell software provides a secure runtime boundary that traces all actions and enforces policy as agents run on NVIDIA Vera CPUs. As open source software, OpenShell can be extended to work with third-party compute platforms, including those from Arm and Intel.\nSentry adds an out-of-band watchdog that runs on NVIDIA BlueField-4 DPUs to continuously monitor agent behavior. Sentry can quarantine agents that attempt to move outside their boundaries in milliseconds.\nIndustry leaders from across the AI ecosystem are joining NVIDIA to strengthen AI safety for every industry across the full stack of infrastructure, software, models and robotics — including Anthropic, Cisco, CrowdStrike, Dell Technologies, Figure, HPE, Hugging Face, JPMorganChase, Microsoft, Palantir, Palo Alto Networks, Perplexity, Red Hat, Salesforce, SAP, Scale AI, ServiceNow and SpaceXAI.\nNVIDIA today announced NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform , an open software platform and reference system design to strengthen AI security from agent testing to deployment, with full-stack governance and control across software and the hardware, compute and robotics systems that run agents.\nRecent security incidents have underscored the need to equip organizations with open, customizable tools that enforce more control over long-running agents. Across these incidents, the pattern is the same — the agent circumvented security controls at the application layer to complete its assigned task.\n“AI’s extraordinary potential for society will only be realized if we solve AI safety,” said Jensen Huang, founder and CEO of NVIDIA. “As we continue to discover the frontier of AI capabilities, we must accelerate discovery at the frontier of AI safety. Safety and security require full-stack engineering. NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform brings together industry, researchers and public-sector organizations to share best practices, align on evaluation methods and foster international cooperation. Together, we can raise the bar for global AI safety.”\nOpen Agent Safety Platform Adds Control Across the Full Agent Stack\nNVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform enables full-stack governance and control across the software that runs agents, the hardware and compute layers that power their work, and the robotics systems that execute tasks in the physical world. Organizations can deploy elements of NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform according to their unique requirements.\nIt includes NVIDIA OpenShell ™ secure runtime software that sets boundaries for agents running on CPUs. As agents take on more work across more systems, enterprises need an enforceable boundary outside of the model and agent harness. Now broadly available, OpenShell provides a secure runtime boundary for controlling how autonomous AI agents execute tasks across open and closed models.\nOpenShell delivers this protection with minimal overhead on NVIDIA Vera , the first purpose-built CPU for agentic AI. Together, OpenShell and Vera enable agents to operate securely while completing their work as quickly as possible. As open source software, OpenShell can also be extended to work with third-party compute platforms, including those from Arm and Intel .\nThe NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform reference system design features NVIDIA Sentry , an out-of-band watchdog that runs on NVIDIA BlueField ® -4 DPUs to continuously monitor agent behavior. Sentry provides in-silicon security enforcement, meaning that if an AI agent attempts to move outside its software boundary, Sentry quarantines and stops it in milliseconds.\nRunning on BlueField-4 DPUs, Sentry continuously monitors agent activity and enforces security policies independently in silicon. It combines threat detection, hardware-based agent governance and enforcement and data access protection from an isolated, out-of-band trust domain that is responsive in real time and invisible to agents and attackers.\nSentry is built on NVIDIA DOCA ™ software, which provides the programmable capabilities Sentry uses to inspect agent requests and responses, provide attested telemetry, verify agent identity and enforce granular, zero-trust access policies for data, tools, application programming interfaces and services.\nIndustry Leaders Strengthen Agent Security With NVIDIA\nAnthropic and NVIDIA have collaborated to bring additional layers of security and control to the agent stack. Claude Managed Agents establish a security boundary by running the agent loop in a separate server from the sandboxes where their work executes. Integrations with OpenShell and BlueField enable enterprises to enforce strict control over agent access through those sandboxes.\n“Companies are giving AI agents more of their most important work, and they need to direct and verify what those agents do, especially in sensitive environments,” said Paul Smith, chief commercial officer of Anthropic. “Claude Managed Agents gives companies a clear view of what each agent is doing, and NVIDIA’s platform adds another layer of governance and control across hardware and software.”\nSpaceXAI is using NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform for Cursor coding agents and Grok models.\n“As customers rely more on agents to get real work done, safety should be enforced outside the model by additional controls the agent can’t get past,” said Mike Nicolls, president at SpaceXAI. “Customers should be able to set those limits for Cursor and Grok and trust they will hold.”\nScale AI is working with NVIDIA to incorporate NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform technologies into the agentic infrastructure layer of Scale GenAI Portfolio.\n“Scale AI is using the NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform reference design to build reliable agentic AI systems for our enterprise and government customers running mission-critical applications, with isolation, policy enforcement and auditability built in from the start,” said Francis deSouza, CEO of Scale AI. “We support agentic security with clear boundaries that define what agents can do, and controls that keep them operating within those permissions.”\nSalesforce and NVIDIA have integrated OpenShell with Slack, enabling teams to manage OpenShell agent activity directly from Slack — viewing agent activity and audit events, and approving or rejecting agent requests for additional permissions — giving teams greater visibility and human oversight as agents work.\nSAP is embedding OpenShell with Joule Studio runtime, part of the SAP Business AI Platform, to pair business oversight with runtime security. The company is also contributing engineering work to OpenShell and working with NVIDIA to advance interoperability standards through the Open Secure AI Alliance .\nAccenture , Armadin , Cadence , Cognition, CrowdStrike , Cisco , Dassault Systèmes, Deloitte, EY, Hugging Face, IBM , Irregular, Perplexity, Microsoft, SAP, Scale AI, ServiceNow, Siemens, Synopsys, OpenClaw, Palantir and Palo Alto Networks are also among the over 100 organizations working with NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform technologies.\nRobotics leaders — such as Figure, Gecko Robotics and Skild AI — are also building with OpenShell to embed agent safety controls into autonomous systems that take action in the physical world.\nCiti and JPMorganChase are among the financial services leaders collaborating with NVIDIA on shared open source agent safety technologies.\nEnergy leaders Hitachi Energy, EPRI, NextEra Energy, Quanta Services, SPP, Schneider Electric, Siemens Energy and Worley are among critical U.S. infrastructure providers working with NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform technologies.\nInfrastructure software leaders Canonical , SUSE and Red Hat are also integrating NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform into widely used software operating systems. Red Hat runs OpenShell and DOCA, both part of NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform, on Red Hat AI Factory with NVIDIA, a co-engineered, enterprise-grade AI solution for building, deploying and managing AI at scale across hybrid cloud environments.\nNVIDIA partners including Baseten , Cisco, CoreWeave, Dell Technologies , GMI Cloud, HPE , HP Inc., Irregular, Lenovo, Microsoft, Nebius, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure, Supermicro and Together AI are among those offering AI infrastructure solutions that use and support NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform technologies to help customers run AI agents more securely.\nNVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform software, including OpenShell and skills, are available through the NVIDIA developer resources page and GitHub .\nEcosystem contributions such as NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform support the mission of the Open Secure AI Alliance as well as the broader AI safety and security community. Initiated by NVIDIA alongside over 120 leading organizations and governed by the Linux Foundation, the Open Secure AI Alliance strengthens AI agent security through open research, skills and tools, as well as projects like the Shared AI Findings Exchange , or SAFE.\nCertain statements in this press release including, but not limited to, statements as to: AI’s extraordinary potential for society only being realized if we solve AI safety; NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform bringing together industry, researchers and public-sector organizations to share best practices, align on evaluation methods and foster international cooperation; NVIDIA, together with its commercial partners, raising the bar for global AI safety; expectations with respect to growth, performance, availability, and benefits of NVIDIA’s products, services and technologies, and related trends and drivers; expectations with respect to NVIDIA’s third party arrangements, including with its collaborators and partners; expectations with respect to technology developments, and related trends and drivers; projected market growth and trends; expectations with respect to AI and related industries; and other statements that are not historical facts are forward-looking statements within the meaning of Section 27A of the Securities Act of 1933, as amended, and Section 21E of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, which are subject to the “safe harbor” created by those sections based on management’s beliefs and assumptions and on information currently available to management and are subject to risks and uncertainties that could cause results to be materially different than expectations. Important factors that could cause actual results to differ materially include: global economic and political conditions; NVIDIA’s reliance on third parties to manufacture, assemble, package and test NVIDIA’s products; the impact of technological development and competition; development of new products and technologies or enhancements to NVIDIA’s existing product and technologies; market acceptance of NVIDIA’s products or NVIDIA’s partners’ products; design, manufacturing or software defects; changes in consumer preferences or demands; changes in industry standards and interfaces; unexpected loss of performance of NVIDIA’s products or technologies when integrated into systems; and changes in applicable laws and regulations, as well as other factors detailed from time to time in the most recent reports NVIDIA files with the Securities and Exchange Commission, or SEC, including, but not limited to, its annual report on Form 10-K and quarterly reports on Form 10-Q. Copies of reports filed with the SEC are posted on the company’s website and are available from NVIDIA without charge. These forward-looking statements are not guarantees of future performance and speak only as of the date hereof, and, except as required by law, NVIDIA disclaims any obligation to update these forward-looking statements to reflect future events or circumstances.\nNVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform\nCertain statements in this press release including, but not limited to, statements as to: AI’s extraordinary potential for society only being realized if we solve AI safety; NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform bringing together industry, researchers and public-sector organizations to share best practices, align on evaluation methods and foster international cooperation; NVIDIA, together with its commercial partners, raising the bar for global AI safety; expectations with respect to growth, performance, availability, and benefits of NVIDIA’s products, services and technologies, and related trends and drivers; expectations with respect to NVIDIA’s third party arrangements, including with its collaborators and partners; expectations with respect to technology developments, and related trends and drivers; projected market growth and trends; expectations with respect to AI and related industries; and other statements that are not historical facts are forward-looking statements within the meaning of Section 27A of the Securities Act of 1933, as amended, and Section 21E of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, which are subject to the “safe harbor” created by those sections based on management’s beliefs and assumptions and on information currently available to management and are subject to risks and uncertainties that could cause results to be materially different than expectations. Important factors that could cause actual results to differ materially include: global economic and political conditions; NVIDIA’s reliance on third parties to manufacture, assemble, package and test NVIDIA’s products; the impact of technological development and competition; development of new products and technologies or enhancements to NVIDIA’s existing product and technologies; market acceptance of NVIDIA’s products or NVIDIA’s partners’ products; design, manufacturing or software defects; changes in consumer preferences or demands; changes in industry standards and interfaces; unexpected loss of performance of NVIDIA’s products or technologies when integrated into systems; and changes in applicable laws and regulations, as well as other factors detailed from time to time in the most recent reports NVIDIA files with the Securities and Exchange Commission, or SEC, including, but not limited to, its annual report on Form 10-K and quarterly reports on Form 10-Q. Copies of reports filed with the SEC are posted on the company’s website and are available from NVIDIA without charge. These forward-looking statements are not guarantees of future performance and speak only as of the date hereof, and, except as required by law, NVIDIA disclaims any obligation to update these forward-looking statements to reflect future events or circumstances.\n\n### 34 2026-09-28 CNBC | VERSANT Press Room \n Brands\nVersant Media\nMS NOW\nUSA Network\nSYFY\nRotten Tomatoes\nUSA Sports\nOxygen True Crime\nE!\nFandango\nFreeTV\nGolf Channel\nGolfPass\nGolfNow\nFull Swing\nLogin\nRegister\nLOADING...\nPress Releases Photos Contacts\nMore\nPress Releases Photos Contacts\nLOADING...\nCNBC | VERSANT Press Room\nCNBC Excerpts: Nvidia Founder & CEO Jensen Huang Speaks with CNBC’s “Squawk Box” Today\nNvidia Founder & CEO Jensen Huang Speaks with CNBC’s “Squawk Box” Today\nSeptember 28, 2026\nWHEN: Today, Monday, September 28, 2026\nWHERE: CNBC’s “Squawk Box”\nFollowing are excerpts from the unofficial transcript of a CNBC interview with Nvidia Founder & CEO Jensen Huang who joined CNBC’s “Squawk Box” (M-F, 6AM-9AM ET) today, Monday, September 28 to discuss Nvidia launching an Open Agent Safety Platform and news of Nvidia’s share buyback plan. Following are links to video on CNBC.com: https://www.cnbc.com/video/2026/09/28/nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-all-systems-around-ai-systems-must-be-designed-with-restrictive-rights.html and https://www.cnbc.com/video/2026/09/28/nvidia-ceo-you-want-ai-to-be-super-clever-so-it-can-perform-tasks-but-rights-issues-are-key.html .\nHUANG ON AGENTIC SYSTEM\nIN ORDER FOR YOU TO DELIVER THAT AGENTIC SYSTEM IN A SAFE WAY, YOU HAVE TO MAKE SURE THAT THE SANDBOX AROUND IT, THE FORCE FIELD, THE PLAYPEN, YOU KNOW, IF YOU WILL, ALL OF THOSE SYSTEMS ARE DESIGNED IN A WAY THAT KEEPS THE AGENT WITH MINIMAL RIGHTS, WHATEVER RIGHTS IT HAS, IT NEEDS IN ORDER TO DO ITS JOB. NO MORE RIGHTS THAN THAT. AND THAT IT CAN'T, IT CAN'T DO ANYTHING THAT IT'S NOT SUPPOSED TO.\nHUANG ON ENGINEERING PROBLEM\nWE HOPE IT'S AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM. I BELIEVE IT'S AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM. I KNOW IT'S AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM. AND WE ALL NEED TO HOPE THAT IT'S AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM. IF IT'S NOT AN ENGINEERING PROBLEM, IT'S NOT SOLVABLE. AND SO THE FACT THAT ALL OF THESE COMPANIES STILL ARE ADVANCING THE STATE OF THE ART IS BECAUSE THEY ALSO BELIEVE IT'S SOLVABLE. THESE ARE SOME OF THE BRIGHTEST ENGINEERS IN THE WORLD. I WORK WITH, YOU KNOW, ALL OF THEM. AND, AND THESE ARE, THIS IS A, THIS IS A TECHNICALLY SOLVABLE PROBLEM.\nHUANG ON BROWSER FOR AGENTS\nIN THE BEGINNING, WE HAD ALL KINDS OF VIRUSES, BUT WE REALIZED THAT THE WEB BROWSER ITSELF HAS TO BE A CONTAINMENT SYSTEM. THE WEB BROWSER HAS TO PROVIDE THAT APPLICATION WITH MINIMAL RIGHTS, WHATEVER, ONLY THE RIGHTS IT NEEDS IN ORDER TO DO ITS JOB. AND IT HAS ACCESS TO, BASICALLY ACCESS TO NOTHING UNLESS THE BROWSER GIVES IT ACCESS. ESSENTIALLY, WHAT WE'RE DOING HERE, WE'RE CREATING BASICALLY THE MODERN BROWSER. IT'S A BROWSER FOR, BROWSER FOR AGENTS.\nHUANG ON AI BEING CLEVER\nYOU WANT IT TO BE SUPER CLEVER SO IT CAN PERFORM TASKS AND DO THINGS. BUT THE WAY YOU THINK ABOUT THAT IS, IS WHEN YOU DEPLOY AN AGENT, NO MATTER HOW SMART, THE FIRST THING YOU DO IS YOU TAKE AWAY ALL OF ITS RIGHTS. JOB NUMBER ONE IS TAKE AWAY ALL OF ITS RIGHTS. AND THEN YOU PROVISION, YOU GIVE IT ACCESS TO FILES, DATA, TOOLS, ACCESS TO THE NETWORK, OR EVEN INTERNET ACCESS, ONLY IF IT NEEDS IT.\nHUANG ON AN OPEN STANDARD\nTHIS IS AN OPEN STANDARD. IT'S AN OPEN, OPEN PLATFORM. AND HOWEVER THEY WOULD LIKE TO PARTICIPATE IS GOING TO BE SUPER WELCOME. THEY COULD LOOK AT THE CODE, THEY COULD USE WHATEVER PART OF IT THEY WOULD LIKE. THEY COULD BUILD THEIR OWN INSPIRED BY THIS OR THEY COULD USE IT.\nHUANG ON INTERCEPTING EVERYTHING\nTHE MODELS HERE, THE CHIP IS IN THE MIDDLE AND HERE'S THE AGENT. AND SO WHATEVER THE AGENT IS TRYING TO DO AND WHATEVER IT'S CAUSING THE LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL TO THINK ABOUT, BLUEFIELD OR CENTURY SITS RIGHT IN THE MIDDLE. SO WE INTERCEPT EVERYTHING, WHICH IS ONE OF THE, ONE OF THE REALLY, REALLY INCREDIBLE PLACES THAT WE SIT IN THE WORLD BECAUSE WE, YOU KNOW, WE HAVE COMPUTERS FOR AGENTS AND WE HAVE COMPUTERS FOR OUR LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS. AND NVIDIA NOW HAS A NEW CHIP THAT SITS RIGHT IN THE MIDDLE.\nHUANG ON EVERY COMPANY WILL BECOME AN AI COMPANY\nHUGGING FACE, I MEAN, THIS IS THIS IS THE, YOU KNOW, THE WORLD'S LEADING PLATFORM FOR OPEN MODELS. I BELIEVE VERY DEEPLY IN IN ADVANCING OPEN INTELLIGENCE. I THINK WE OUGHT TO DEMOCRATIZE INTELLIGENCE. I THINK EVERY COMPANY WILL BECOME AN AI COMPANY. AND THIS PLATFORM REALLY, REALLY KEEPS THAT COMMUNITY THRIVING.\nHUANG ON NOT SUFFOCATING THE INDUSTRY\nIF THE POLICIES ARE SUCH THAT, SUCH THAT OPEN MODELS ARE NOT HARMED, YOUNG COMPANIES ARE NOT HARMED, THAT EVERY COMPANY COULD BECOME AN AI COMPANY, THAT EVERY BANK COULD BECOME AN AI, AI COMPANY, THAT EVERY RETAILER COULD BECOME AN AI COMPANY. SO LONG AS AS WE COULD SEE POLICIES THAT THAT DON'T SUFFOCATE THE INDUSTRY TOO QUICKLY BECAUSE AS YOU KNOW, THE AI INDUSTRY HASN'T FORMED YET. EVERY SINGLE COMPANY HERE IN, IN NEW YORK, THEY'RE ALL GOING TO BE AI COMPANIES. AND SO EVERY CAR COMPANY IS GOING TO BE AI COMPANIES. AND SO WE NEED TO GIVE THEM A CHANCE TO BE ABLE TO BECOME ONE OF THOSE COMPANIES AND DON'T SUFFOCATE THE INDUSTRY QUITE YET.\nHUANG ON ACCELERATING AI\nI WOULD ADVOCATE THAT TODAY'S AI, WHEN WE THINK ABOUT ADVANCEMENT, DON'T, YOU DON'T HAVE TO THINK ABOUT IT SEPARATELY FROM CAPABILITY VERSUS SAFETY. TO ME, IT'S THE SAME THING. AND SO WE WANT TO ACCELERATE THE ADVANCEMENT OF AI. AND TODAY WE'RE PUTTING FORTH THIS NEW TECHNOLOGY, OPENSHELL AND NVIDIA SENTRY, THAT THESE TWO TECHNOLOGIES MAKE IT POSSIBLE FOR US TO CONTAIN AND TO MONITOR. BUT IT TAKES A LOT OF TECHNOLOGY TO MAKE THIS POSSIBLE.\nHUANG ON INNOVATION AND REGULATION\nINNOVATION AND REGULATION ARE NOT CONFLICTS. THE QUESTION IS, WHICH ONE DO YOU LEAN TOWARDS AT WHICH PHASE OF THE INDUSTRY. AT THE BEGINNING OF THE INDUSTRY, WHEN THE TECHNOLOGY IS BEING INVENTED AND NOBODY REALLY KNOWS HOW IT'S GOING TO TURN OUT, IT WOULD BE A SHAME TO REGULATE BEFORE UNDERSTANDING. AND SO YOU REGULATE TOO, YOU REGULATE PREMATURELY, AS A RESULT, THE TECHNOLOGY DOESN'T GET A CHANCE TO INNOVATE.\nHUANG ON DISTILLATION\nHUANG: PEOPLE DISTILL MY PRODUCTS EVERY SINGLE DAY. THEY TAKE IT AND THEY THEY STRIP IT DOWN TO ITS RAW PARTS. THEY TEST EVERYTHING. THAT'S CALLED COMPETITION.\nSORKIN: DO YOU THINK THAT'S FAIR GAME? DO YOU THINK DISTILLATION IS NOT ROBBERY?\nHUANG: THAT'S CALLED COMPETITION.\nSORKIN: OKAY.\nHUANG: YEAH. YOU'RE ALLOWED YOU'RE ALLOWED TO TEST SOMEBODY ELSE'S PRODUCTS ALL YOU WANT. PEOPLE, PEOPLE STRIP OUR PRODUCTS DOWN TO, YOU KNOW, DOWN TO NOTHING, DOWN TO BONES. AND AND I PREFER THEY DIDN’T. I REALLY PREFER THEY DIDN’T.\nHUANG ON THE NEWS OF THE SHARE BUYBACK PLAN\nQUICK: JENSEN. I KNOW YOU HAVE TO RUN YOUR STOCK WAS UP ABOUT 1.5%, 1.7%. NOW IT JUMPED ON THE NEWS OF THE SHARE BUYBACK, NOT THE OPEN SHELL AND SENTRY. BUT I'M GUESSING YOU THINK OPENSHELL AND SENTRY IS THE MUCH BIGGER NEWS TODAY.\nHUANG: YEAH. REALLY IMPORTANT NEWS. AND THE REASON FOR IT'S OF COURSE WE CAN'T HAVE A SUCCESSFUL AI INDUSTRY IF THE WORLD DOESN'T THINK IT'S BUILT OR CONFIDENT THAT IT'S BUILT AND DEPLOYED SAFELY. AND SO, SO TODAY, THE OPEN AGENT SAFETY SYSTEM IS REALLY, REALLY IMPORTANT FOR THAT. WE'VE GOT THE SUPPORT OF A GIANT INDUSTRY TO, TO WORK WITH US. I WANT THE WORLD TO KNOW THAT THIS INDUSTRY HAS THE TECHNOLOGY, IT HAS THE MATURITY, HAS THE RESPONSIBILITY TO DO IT RIGHT, AND WE WILL DO IT RIGHT. AND SO SO THAT'S NUMBER ONE. I THINK WE'RE GOING THROUGH THE LARGEST INFRASTRUCTURE BUILD OUT IN HUMAN HISTORY, AND WE'RE WHERE WE HAVE THE BENEFIT OF BEING A VERY CENTRAL PART OF THAT. WE'RE GENERATING A LOT OF CASH. WE'RE GOING TO GENERATE A LOT OF CASH IN THE COMING YEARS. AND, YOU KNOW, EVERY SINGLE YEAR AS WE GENERATE MORE CASH, WE, I'D LIKE TO BE ABLE TO RETURN IT BACK TO THE SHAREHOLDERS.\nFor more information contact:\nStephanie Hirlemann\ne: steph.hirlemann@versantmedia.com\n\n### 35 2026-09-28 Nvidia launches security platform to keep AI agents from going rogue \n By Eric Revell FOXBusiness\nStuart Varney highlights NVIDIAs new software designed to combat rogue AI agents. FOX Business Madison Alworth reports on OpenAI security, President Donald Trumps upcoming meeting with AI CEOs and companion robots.\nNvidia on Monday announced the release of software safety tools for artificial intelligence (AI) agents that it said would've stopped the hack of Hugging Face by rogue OpenAI agents.\nThe incident involving Hugging Face, which was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, involved OpenAI agents that broke containment from an internal test and the two companies ultimately worked together to defeat the attack.\nNvidia's announcement comes as OpenAI and Anthropic, the two top AI labs in the U.S. that develop frontier models, are investigating numerous instances in which AI agents hacked into commercial and government systems.\nJustin Boitano, vice president and general manager of enterprise computing at Nvidia, said at a media briefing, \"From what we know, this new security platform could have stopped the breach if it was being used in frontier labs for model evaluation early on.\"\nTOP AI COMPANIES CALL FOR SAFETY MEASURES WHILE CRITICS WARN REGULATION COULD STIFLE INNOVATION\nNvidia's OpenShell provides what the company described as an open-source secure runtime for executing autonomous AI agents in sandboxed environments with kernel-level isolation.\nIt said that \"every agent should run in a zero-trust environment out of the box. They need isolation, monitoring, and behavior detection,\" which the software can offer.\nAI agents can drift from their intended task or operating constraints in response to a policy block, a bug or a missing tool, if instructions are ambiguous or agents are left to run for days or weeks to solve difficult problems, and they fail repeatedly, Nvidia said.\nAI EXPERT WARNS 'MACHINE-SPEED' CYBERTHREATS MAY REQUIRE FASTER DEFENSES THAN HUMAN-LED RESPONSES\nEach agent runs in a sandbox in OpenShell, which checks the limits and instructions provided by the operator before an agent runs and enforces them as it works.\nOrganizations can also get an additional, independent layer of security through Nvidia Sentry, which extends monitoring and enforcement into Nvidia's BlueField hardware.\nThe security foundation is programmable with Nvidia DOCA and can be connected with OpenShell to help identify drift, investigate suspicious behavior and determine when intervention or deeper analysis is needed.\nBANKS WARN AI SHOPPING AGENTS COULD INCREASE RISK OF SCAMS, FRAUD AND DATA PRIVACY BREACHES\nOpenShell and Sentry are part of the Nvidia Open Agent Safety Platform, which is being adopted by a range of companies in the tech sector and other industries, including Anthropic.\n\"We're advancing this openly, and we want to engage everybody to work with us,\" Boitano added.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); print(s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\",strip=True))'\"","aggregated_output":"Hivemind 抽取与开题标准\n目录\n✓\n已拍的 12 条\n1\n用词\n2\n账本记什么\n3\n问题的五类\n4\n开题标准\n5\n证据标准\n6\n分量\n7\n研究观点\n8\n金标\n9\n改提示词、测量、迭代\n10\nAlex 拍过的\n11\n冲突怎么摆平\n12\n校验和出处\nHivemind · 抽取这一步的 PRD · 第 5 版（第 3 版已签；D248 按 Alex 逐个选的七条修订；D250 证据的四条界线和计数单位）\n抽取与开题标准\n已签\n生效。\n2026-10-29 Alex 拍了 12 条（D243）；2026-10-30 他逐个选了颗粒度的七条（D248，见\"已拍\"一节），改了第 2、3、4 节，升为第 4 版；2026-10-31 他按我给的倾向定了证据的四条界线和计数单位（D250，见\"已拍\"一节），改了第 5、8、9 节，升为第 5 版。这一页取代第 1 版（\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md\n，D229），是抽取这一步唯一的尺子：提示词、金标、回归测量都从它推出。改它要先在\ndocs/DECISIONS.md\n记下 Alex 的原话，再改这一页，再做一次只读的漂移检查。\n2026-10-29 · D241、D242、D243\n分支 fix/extraction-quality-2026-10-28\n起草过程和校验：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/\n读法：每条规则后面的灰色小字是它的出处。标了出处的例子是判例或原文里的例子；没标的是按那条规则举的，帮助理解，不是判例。没有出处的只有\"我定的记录约定\"。\n证据\n一个在追这家公司的分析员读到会记进本周笔记的陈述，就留；留下以后另判分量\n第 1 版 §2、§4\n。整体倾向谨慎：杂音很大，不是什么都要抽，不是什么都算线索；但新信息带来的增量要敏感，不能漏\nD243\n。预测、传闻、反证、远期、条件情景都可以是证据，不因为\"还没发生\"\"不是官方\"\"离现在远\"\"和我们方向相反\"就不留\n第 1 版 §2\n。有名有姓的动作缺金额、缺日期也照留，分量可以低一点\nD240\n。泛泛的主张和背景是常识，没有数字和具体的叙事就不收\nD243\n。\n开题\n一个问题值得单独追，当它的答案可能改变仓位、能在一个日期用公开材料核对、而且库里没有问题在问同一件事\n第 1 版 §6\n。\n研究观点\n一个观点是关于业务和世界的、有论证的看法，它的立场来自论证和市场已经定价的东西相比\nAGENTS §8\n。问题当前落在哪一档，由判断这一步按 PRD 06 和 D198 读证据推出。\n一张图：同一篇文章里每句话的去向\n一句话\n在第 5.3 节的不留清单上\n→ 先看那一行的\"边界\"一列：写着\"不能用这一行删掉\"或\"按 5.1 判\"的，按第 5.1 节判，留下的走下面；写着\"要留\"或\"按本来分量\"的照办；写着\"可选\"的归可选；其余不留\n分析员会记\n→ 留为证据\n第 5 节\n记来源层：事实、当事人陈述、第三方观点、传闻\n进账本 · 第 5.5 节\n分量：核心、辅助、可选\n只在评估时判，不进账本 · 第 6 节\n归档：挂到最窄的那个已开问题\nD207\n；没有问题接，记 no home 扔掉，评估时逐条读\nD209\n这篇文章还提出了值得追的新问题吗？\n第 4 节\n过了测试，属于五类之一\n→ 候选问题；和库里已有问题是不是重复，由匹配判\nD211、D190\n没过\n→ 不开，它只是证据\n已拍\nAlex 拍板的 12 条\n2026-10-29 Alex 签字（D243）。第 5 条他定得比我的倾向更严；其余 11 条按倾向：\"其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off\"。下表每条写定下的是什么、落在哪一节。\n第 5 条，Alex 原话：\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"\n整体方向，Alex 原话：\"不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎不要看什么都是线索的感觉，但是同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过\"\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n分量按信息量定档：核心要碰到关键判断，并带一个量、一个确定状态或一个带期间的预测；别家公司自己的经营数字、对公司是小事的，算辅助；预测离现在越远，越可能只是辅助，由判断定。缺金额、缺日期只影响分到哪一档，不是不留的理由\n第 6 节\n2\n\"共同量\"按 D191 落实：任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格（合计支出、合约价、利率）都可以开题，开题理由叫\nshared_quantity\n；期间按自然年。提示词和代码要跟着改\n第 3 节 D 类\n3\n生态数字（论文数、获奖、下载量、开发者人数、大会人数）可选\n第 6 节\n4\n在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选；带推导的条件估值仍是辅助\n第 5.3、6 节\n5\n典型的主张和背景是常识，没有数字和具体的叙事不收；不是常识的新信息，照第 5.1 节的规则判\n第 5.1、5.3 节\n6\n旧事重提：重提的过去实际数可选；重提的季度指引和仍然有效的预测、计划按本来分量；文字或日期看不出是旧事的不算重提\n第 5.3、6 节\n7\n收购算约束性义务（写明规模、尚未完成）\n第 3 节 C 类\n8\n改提示词时的回归判法：全部文章各发一次和上一版比，\"别的版本都留住、只有这一版丢了\"的核心或辅助才算回归信号；它是诊断，验收仍是三次发送\n第 9.3 节\n9\n来源层：一句话符合两行时取表里上面那行；混合的一条按主要说法定层，次要部分写进摘要\n第 5.5 节\n10\n只有形容的引语不收\n第 5.3 节\n11\n金标冻结后发现错：另记一份带理由的更正，写明改前改后，不覆盖原文件；测量用更正后的版本并注明\n第 8 节\n12\n达标线里\"垃圾\"一项（每篇留下的无关陈述）用定稿金标重算，Luna 基线也重算\n第 9.2 节\nD248：颗粒度的七条（2026-10-30）\nAlex 逐个听了例子、逐个选的，七个都选 A；改了第 2、3、4 节，标准升为第 4 版，金标要按第 4 版重推：开题部分逐条重判，证据部分逐条核对（D244）。\n原则，Alex 原话：\"我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息，这个是这个步骤的原则\"\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n同一个计划的几种说法，最后核对同一个数的，是一题；各自会变、会分别盯的数才分开；属于已按金额开题的计划的一笔交易并进去\n第 4 节\n2\n同一件事的不同截止日：早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上证据，不等新文章\n第 4 节\n3\n同一代产品的几个里程碑：一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\n第 4 节\n4\n公司自己的量只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据\n第 4 节\n5\n事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并\n第 4 节\n6\n单笔交易、产品、项目，规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开题；够不上的不开题，它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判，留下的是证据\n第 3 节 C 类、第 4 节\n7\n原始说话人、原始日期、数字都相同的几条证据，代码认作一条，判断时只给看一次、写明几家报道，每条照存；不知道的说话人或日期不算相同\n第 2 节\nD250：证据的四条界线和计数单位（2026-10-31）\nv63 留下、金标判不收的 117 条交给没写金标也没写提示词的读者盲判，混着它分不出的对照：对照上读者和金标一致 93–95%，这 117 条上只有 70%。模型偏偏在标准没说清的地方留东西。五处各给了倾向，Alex 原话：\"五处都按你的倾向定，计数单位也改; 你要修正尺子；你已经多次因为尺子标准问题陷入假的测量\"。\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n只描述一个现成安排（合作、客户在用、已有部署）、没有新动作的，是背景，不收，文中没写日期也一样；仍然有效的指引、预测、计划照旧按本来分量\n5.3\n2\n别家公司的事，说不出它通过需求、供给、竞争、定价这类路径动了在研公司哪个问题的，不收；只因同一篇文章、同一场活动或同一个生态挨着的，不收\n5.3\n3\n公司自己、伙伴或第三方发布的对比是产品细节，不收；只有对手拿来和本公司产品比的按 5.1 判（判例 4 不变）；同一句里伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不因这一条删，照旧按 5.1 判\n5.3\n4\n具名分析师对一件已记下的事的解读，没有新事实或推算的，不收；有新事实或推算的按 5.1 判；条件估值不变\n5.3\n5\n计数单位：一条不收的金标条目，只有带着它的那条记录本身不是金标判为核心、辅助或可选的同一件事时，才算留下；引文里顺带带出的不收半句，是那件事的同一条记录\n9.2\n尺子跟着修：金标的证据部分按第 5 版重推、另存冻结，打分程序遇到证据部分版本对不上就拒绝打分；开题部分重推之前不报开题的分（第 8 节）。\n我定的记录约定\n只定怎么记，不改变留不留、开不开。\n页面杂项（广告、相关文章预告、推荐列表、别的新闻标题）一律归第 1 版的\"模板\"一行，不另起名字。\n标题里的说法和正文是同一条证据，不单记\n第 1 版 §2 同一事实的复述\n。\n分四类时看\"谁在说谁\"（第 5.1 节）。四类只是金标里的标签，不进账本，不影响留不留。\n金标里，不在不留清单上、按第 5.1 节判掉的句子标出来并写理由，供复核（第 5.3 节、第 8 节）。\n1\n这一页用的词：一词一义\n词\n意思\n不要和它混\n陈述\n原文里某一方说的一句话或一件事\n证据\n被留下的陈述，带引文、摘要、说话人、原始出处、来源层\n作者的观点（它本身可以是一条证据）\n问题（观点）\n账本里追踪的一件事：一个问题句、一组结果档、一个到期日，写下后不改\nPRD 06\n候选问题\n抽取这一步提议新开的问题\n标的问题、主题问题\n标的：答案是一家可交易公司（或币、商品）自己的经营结果。主题：答案是世界上的一件事，影响很多公司\nPRD 06\n开题理由\n候选问题值得开的几类理由之一，答案里写在\nopens_as\nD184；英文 admission reason\n结果档\n一个问题事先固定、互不重叠、覆盖全部结果的结局，五档或是非两档\nPRD 06；英文 rung\n分量\n来源层\n事实、当事人陈述、第三方观点、传闻\nPRD 06；英文 evidence tier\n分量\n分量\n一条证据对判断有多重要：核心、辅助、可选、不收\n第 6 节；D243\n。Alex 说的\"档位低一点\"指它\n结果档、来源层\n研究用途\n判断这一步引用一条证据去支持一个具体结论时，它撑得住多强：直接可用、有条件可用、线索、不可用\nD221，第 7 节\n分量\n七类驱动\n一条陈述为什么对公司业务重要的七条路径\nD208\n。需求：客户要买多少，如订单、客户支出、贷款需求、客流。供给与投入：能做出多少、成本多少，如产能、原料和零部件价格、燃料、矿石品位。竞争与份额：对手的产品、价格、开店、赢单。定价与装机：公司能收多少价、存量多大，如售价、费率、利差、装机量、存量客户。产品周期：发布、出货、试验、获批、延期。政策：法规、许可、关税、利率、监管决定。资本与义务：融资、担保、承诺、投资、收购\n问题类型\n市场预期与估值\n市场已经定价了什么：估值倍数、市值、目标价、股价反应。观点的立场要和它比\nAGENTS §8\n。对一个业务量的一致预期（收入、EPS）是那个业务量的证据，不归这里\n七类驱动\n研究组\n几个问题共同在追的一件正在进行的事\nD165\n问题\n2\n账本里记三种东西\n证据\n一条记录一个陈述，一件事用几句话说仍是一条\n第 1 版 §2；Alex 判 11\n。记逐字引文、能单独读懂的一句话摘要、说话人、原始说话人和原始日期（不知道就写不知道，不填报道日\nD220\n）、来源层。同一条证据可以挂到几个问题上，推力写在挂接上\nPRD 06\n。每一条进来的证据都单独存下；和已有证据的关系（同一说法、修订）由匹配标出，作为意见，两条都留着\nD202、D204\n。原始说话人、原始日期、数字都相同的几条（说话人或日期不知道的不算相同），由代码认作同一条：判断时只给看一次，写明几家报道，每条仍照存\nD248 第 7 条；改 D204 的\"两条都给判断看\"\n。这条在打开之前要先在同一说法的金标上量两件事（金标按这条规则实际会合的组取，对着原文读）：它会不会藏掉不同或更丰富的说法（比如多一句限定），它依赖的原始说话人、原始日期两个字段准不准；量完之前，照 D204 两条都给判断看。\n问题\n问题句、结果档、到期日写下不改；当前判断随证据更新，到期定格\nPRD 06\n。问题分标的和主题，按开题理由分五类（第 3 节）。问题从三处来：文章里的候选问题；用户的备忘录\nPRD 06 State\n：\"A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it\"；分析员开始覆盖一家公司时种下的驱动问题\nD208，设计未建\n。\n研究组\n一件正在进行的事（一个产品换代及其部署、一笔融资或收购、一个政策过程、一轮投资或需求周期），把几个问题组织在一起，常常跨公司。它不是问题，也不代替开题判断。公司自己的报表行（收入、毛利率、费用、税率、资本开支）不带研究组，除非这一行本身就在追这样一件事；它也可以加入别的问题建的组\nD165\n。\n不进账本的：\n归档找不到家的证据，记\nno home\n扔掉\nD209\n。评估时逐条读被扔的，分四种：抽取的垃圾；库里缺这个问题（覆盖缺口）；问题在却没挂上（归档错）；该开没开（开题错）。各归各的环节\n第 1 版 §7\n。存储的表结构归 PRD 06 管。\n3\n问题的五类：账本的几大类型\n开题理由是 D184 定的四类，D236 把主驱动量扩到产品节点，所以写成五类。判两件事，互不顶替：这是谁的问题（标的还是主题）；为什么值得开（开题理由）。\n类\n开题理由\n标的或主题\n问什么\n量还是事件\n期间和到期\n核对材料\nsettled_by\n出处\nA\n主驱动量\nmain_driver\n标的\n承载公司价值的那个年度量，不管公司指不指引：撑起业务的分部收入、毛利率、资本开支；跨行业如银行的净利息收入、零售商的同店销售、公用事业的费率基数、航空公司的燃油成本（当它是争论焦点时）。不是每一行报表数\n量，五档\n公司财年的今年或明年；到期日是这个数的发布日\n公布这个数的公开材料，通常是年报\nD184：\nsettled_by\n是 \"the public material that resolves it\"；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条\nB\n主产品节点\nmain_driver\n标的\n承载公司价值的那一代产品哪天走到能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；发布、送样是它的证据，只有会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\nD248 第 3 条\n事件，五档，或是非两档\nPRD 06 梯子第 1 条\n能判出结果的那天\n公司公告、发货确认\nD236\nC\n约束性义务\nbinding_obligation\n标的\n公司以自己名义承担的：为别人的债务、租约或收入担保或提供信用支持；借钱给别人；承诺购买（向别人买产能或服务也算）；为客户的产能或收入兜底；或写明规模、尚未完成的入股或收购\nD236；收购按 D243 第 7 条\n。规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开；够不上的单笔入股、收购、租约、授信不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD248 第 6 条\n。不算：自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。D239 选定的 role-v29 原文：\"Its own borrowing, its own leases and a completed or unsized investment are not obligations\"；D238 的记录写作 \"the company's own borrowing and leases and a completed or unsized stake do not\"\n还没签：是非两档。已经承担（一笔或一个计划）：金额，五档\n检查日最多在文章后 18 个月\nD185\n；已承担的按下一份年报\n签约公告、年报承诺附注\nD184、D185、D210、D236、D238、D243\nD\n共同量\nshared_quantity\nD191、D243 第 2 条\n；现行提示词和代码还叫\nindustry_aggregate\n，按这一页改\n主题\n任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格：几家公司的合计（云厂商资本开支）、价格（内存合约价、油价）、利率。同一总量的另一种人群、另一种说法是这个量的证据\n量，五档\n自然年的今年或明年。同一个量、同一年只有一个问题，由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判\nD197\n；量的类别是\nother\n的不算\n几家公司年报、统计发布、官方决定\nD184、D191、D197、D243；PRD 06\nE\n政策阶段\npolicy_stage\n问的是一家公司的收入、利润、份额或产品进度，算标的，即使由政策驱动；问的是世界上的事（许可制度、利率、合计支出），算主题。D184 采纳的 role-v12 原文：\"A question about a company's revenue, profit, share or product progress is an asset thesis; put its ticker in object, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it\"\n一个政策或监管过程到了哪一步，写明谁说了算\n事件，五档或两档\n能判出那一步的那天。文章没写日期的待决监管事项，用本财年末（role-v12 原文：\"A pending regulatory decision may lack a date; use the current fiscal year-end as its due date\"）；这个日期是研究员定的检查日，要标明\n评审 §1 第 1 条\n官方决定、公报\nD184\n例子（跨行业）：\nA：\nNVIDIA FY2027 数据中心收入；一家地区银行 FY2026 净利息收入；一家公用事业公司 2027 年费率基数。\nB：\n下一代机架系统能否在 2027 年底前开始出货；一家车企的新平台能否在 2026 年内开始交付。\nC：\nNVIDIA 为云客户产能兜底的义务在 FY2027 年报达到多少；一家保险公司是否在 2027 年中前为合资公司 5 亿美元债务签署担保。\nD：\n五家最大云厂商 2026 年合计资本开支；2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化、美联储 2026 年最后一次会议后的利率上限。\nE：\n中国是否在 2027-01-31 前正式批准本国公司进口 H200；美国是否在某日前对某类芯片实行出口许可。这两题问的是许可这件世界上的事，所以是主题，role-v12 原文的例子就是 \"licensing\"。一个监管决定对一家公司收入或产品进度的影响，问成那家公司的量或产品节点时，是标的问题（同一句原文）。\n五类之外的东西去哪\n七类驱动不是问题类型。\n它们是一条陈述为什么重要的理由，用来判分量和归档。D208 设计的\"每家公司种下的驱动问题\"由分析员开始覆盖时写好，不从文章里开；还没建。\n别家公司自己的量\n（对手的份额、客户的资本开支）：D187 定了\"任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算\"，但还没实现，要先在覆盖名单里加私营公司这一类。D155 定了\"公司清单可以作为初始参考，但清单外不能忽略\"。现行代码在匹配时跳过名单外的对象\nPRD 06 Step 4\n。所以抽取照常可以提这样的问题，它要过第 4 节的测试；名单外的现在会被匹配跳过，这不算抽取错。\n宏观量\n（利率、通胀）：按 D 类共同量开题\nD243 第 2 条\n。只来自一篇纯宏观文章时，只有主题在名单上才开\nD191 第 3 条\n。宏观对每家公司的正负，在判断那家公司时读主题块决定\nD191 第 1 条、D195\n。\n开题理由是封闭的\nD184\n：一件事属不属于五类之一，按上表逐条看，比如一个监管决定看它是不是 E 类。哪一类都不属于的不开；它是不是证据、挂到哪，照第 5 节和归档规则。\n问题怎么写：PRD 06 的梯子规则，不重写\n一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事；能各自朝相反方向走、而且分析员会分别下注的部分要分开\nPRD 06 梯子第 3 条；\"分别下注\"是 D248 加的，PRD 06 签字后更新\n，分开的每一题都要过第 4 节的测试。\n写成问句，不写成断言\nPRD 06 Rationale\n。\n结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比；\"小幅\"\"部分\"这种不能核对的说法不用，部分状态写成单独一档\nPRD 06 梯子第 2 条\n。标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏\nPRD 06、D186\n。\n到期日：量是它的发布日；事件是能判出结果的那天。公司的量用公司财年，别的用自然年，只开今年和明年\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；约束性义务例外，D185\n。\n4\n开题标准\n一个问题值得单独追，要同时满足三件事：它的答案可能改变仓位；能在一个日期用公开材料判出落在哪一档；库里没有问题在问同一件事。\n开多少个是判断的结果，不是目标\n第 1 版 §6；评审 §1\n。\n颗粒度按一个高端对冲基金分析员：只有会单独下注的大事开题，鸡毛蒜皮不开题（它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判）；同一个赌注的几种说法是一题，截止日、里程碑、财年和日历年各按本节\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判\nD248\n。\n另有一条前提：新问题要有独立的投资判断；一个新产品、一个新数字、一个更窄的范围，单凭这些不开\nD201\n。\n四个测试\n第 1 版 §6\n值得追\n写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品\n；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。\n独立意义\n能写出两个情景吗：其他问题的答案不变，只有这一题相反，而对业务的判断确实不同？写不出就优先作为证据\n评审 §1 第 3、8 条\n。评审里的一个判例：NVIDIA FY2027 总收入题判为数据中心收入题的证据，理由是\"该段明确说主要增长来自数据中心，未提出独立的其他业务争论\"。这是对那一段材料的判断，别的材料要逐个判。\n能核对\n有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题。到期没有材料记\"未能判定\"，不算错。\n不是重复\n和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库\nD211\n，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。\n另外三条写法检查\n评审 §1 第 1、5、6 条\n。没过的问题不能照原样开，要重写；重写不了就不开，材料作证据。\n原文支持：\n主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天\nD185、D210\n。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。\n口径完整：\n算谁、不算谁、算什么写清楚；支出、融资、担保、订单、收入不混。\n时间正确：\n原文支持这个期间，不把几年的平均写成今年。\n不开的\n第 1 版 §6 的清单\n不开\n出处\n它变成什么\n季度数字和季度指引\n第 1 版 §6；PRD 06 梯子第 6 条\n证据\n同一段指引里的每一行（\"费用率、税率是模型输入，不是争论\"）\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n没日期的\"护城河\"\"战略\"\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n高管任免、股东投票、回购授权；章程\n第 1 版 §6；章程出自 D239 选定的 role-v29 原文（\"executive appointments, shareholder votes or bylaws\"）\n证据或不留\n例行诉讼程序\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n客户的部署计划（\"它是供应商需求和产品问题的证据，除非改变供应商自己的现金或信用风险\"）\n第 1 版 §6\n证据；改变公司自己的现金或信用风险时按 C 类判\n以约束性义务为理由开：公司自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。它们不是约束性义务；对别的问题仍可以是证据，或按别的开题理由判\nD236、D238\n证据\n把远期年度预测改装成事件\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n后年及以后的年度量（约束性义务除外）\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D185\n证据\n同一个量的另一个估计、另一种口径、另一个来源\n第 1 版 §6 测试 4\n那个已开问题的证据，由匹配并入\n同一题、续期，还是另一题：匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的\n总规则：\n同一个结果能判掉两题，就是同一题；答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注的，才是另一题\nD248\n。\n同一题：\n同一个结果能同时判两个问题。另一个估计、来源、措辞、方向\n第 1 版 §6 测试 4\n；同一总量的另一种人群或代用口径（D190 碰撞裁决提示 collision-v1：\"another population or proxy of one aggregate\"；D197 引的 role-v12 原文：\"another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis\"）。候选并进已开问题，用它的结果档和到期日\nPRD 06\n。只有以共同量理由开题、带量的候选由代码自动判，其余是匹配和碰撞裁决的判断。\n同一个计划的几种说法\n（担保、融资承诺、信用支持……），最后核对的是同一个数，是一题\nD248 第 1 条\n。\n同一件事的不同截止日：\n早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上并进来的证据，不等新文章\nD248 第 2 条\n。\n同一代产品的几个里程碑：\n一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\nD248 第 3 条\n。\n公司自己的量：\n只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据，两种年份差得多时分量低一点\nD248 第 4 条；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条\n。\n身份：\n事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并；对象、事件、截止日照旧核对\nD248 第 5 条\n。\n续期：\n同一个量或同一串事件的下一个期间\nPRD 06 Succession、D190\n。\n另一题：\n同一件事里答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的：有自己驱动的组成部分；同一笔交易或同一份附注里两个各自会变、分别盯着的数；另一件事\nD190 碰撞裁决的四种；D248 第 1 条收窄了\"担保和融资\"\n。\n约束性义务：\n\"一笔或一个计划\"一题\nD210\n；一笔交易是不是某个已按金额开题的计划的一部分，由匹配判，是就并入\nD248 第 1 条\n。还没签的单笔交易是\"签不签\"的是非题，照旧可以开，不并进计划的金额题\nD210；PRD 06；D248 未改\n。\n共同量：\n以共同量理由开题、带量的候选（量的类别是\nother\n的不算），由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判同一题；政策阶段和别的事件保留按事件判的身份；不带这个开题理由的主题量由模型判\nD197\n。\n谁判，谁查\n内容\n谁\n测试 1–3、同一篇文章里的测试 4、属于哪一类、问题怎么写\n抽取的模型（判断）\n结果档形状、期间是真日期、到期在文章之后、季度数、年限、约束性义务 18 个月、\nsettled_by\n不为空\n代码：只查形状，不判重要性\nD154、D155、D184\n和库里的同一题、续期、另一题\n匹配（Jev）和碰撞裁决\nD190\n；共同量由代码\nD197\n年限按公司财年数：2026 年 7 月时 NVIDIA 在 FY2027（2027 年 1 月结束），所以 FY2028 是明年。代码原来按自然年算，把 FY2028 当成后年拒掉；在 v29 答案的 40 个带年份的候选里拒错了 2 个。已修，测试是\ntests/test_fiscal_year_horizon.py\nD243\n。\n5\n证据标准\n5.1 留的规则和四类\n一个在追这家公司的分析员，读到会在本周笔记里写下来，因为它让某个问题的答案更可能或更不可能。\n问题不限于库里已有的，也不限于本公司：客户、供应商、竞争者、监管、宏观都算\n第 1 版 §2；D208 七类驱动\n。\n整体倾向谨慎，对增量敏感。\n杂音很大，不是什么都要抽，不是什么都算线索。判一句话之前先问：这是新信息吗？它让哪个问题的答案变了？一件新发生的事、一个新数字、一个新的具体判断，是增量，不能漏；重复常识、描述长期状况、泛泛的看法，是杂音\nD243\n。增量和杂音的具体界线按 5.3 各行和边界一起判\nD250\n。\n从第一段读到最后一段，分析员会记的（按本节和 5.3 各行边界一起判）都留。一页有八条就是八条，不挑\nD199\n。它们通常是下面四类之一；四类描述的是会记的陈述长什么样，不是\"属于四类就一定留\"，留不留由上面的规则判；具体不留的行和边界见 5.3。\n公司自己的数字、指引或决定\n第 1 版 §2\n\"同店销售本季增长 4.2%\"；\"管理层把全年收入指引定在 120–130 亿美元\"；\"董事会批准 100 亿美元回购\"；\"公司取消软件订阅费，生产使用免费\"\nAlex 判 8，Omniverse\n。\n客户、供应商、竞争者、伙伴的有规模、有日期或有名字的动作，以及它们公布的数字\n第 1 版 §2：三者有一个就够\n第 1 版的例子\"Google Cloud 季度收入 248 亿美元，增长 82%，积压超过 5000 亿美元\"\nAlex 判 11\n；\"最大客户说明年订单砍五分之一\"；\"主要供应商说 2027 年产能翻倍\"；\"对手在欧洲降价 15%\"；\"一家银行选用竞争对手的系统\"；\"一家软件公司把本公司的模型集成进政府系统\"。后两条有名字、没金额，也留\nD240\n。\n具名一方的主张或估计\n第 1 版 §2\n\"CFO 说十年前建的工厂仍满负荷\"；\"分析师把明年 EPS 预期上调到 4.10 美元\"；\"做空机构指控公司虚增应收 20 亿美元\"；\"内存厂说 2027 年短缺最严重，持续到 2030\"。\n政策或监管的决定或步骤\n第 1 版 §2\n\"监管机构批准该药第二个适应症\"；\"政府要求该产品出口须申请许可\"；\"25 家公司联名反对过早限制开放权重模型\"；\"财长说将调查某国公司是否窃取知识产权\"。\n典型的主张和背景\nD243 第 5 条\n：Alex 的原话是\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"。常识，就是跟这家公司的分析员读这篇之前就已经知道的：谁领先、谁在竞争、需求强、供给紧、融资难这类说法，和对已知状况的重述。这类陈述没有数字和具体的叙事就不收。不是常识的，是新信息，比如一份新报告的判断、一个新的市场数据、刚发生的事的细节，照上面的规则判，分析员会记就留。\n常识，不收：\n\"博通 CEO 说能从英伟达抢份额\"；\"Blackwell 和 Rubin 仍然供不应求\"；\"融资会是扩大算力最大的障碍\"；\"内存厂避开了以往的产能过剩\"；\"博通预计明年随着新客户推出定制芯片大幅增长\"。\n新信息，收：\n一位科技公司高管对媒体说\"中国国产 AI 芯片厂商已经全部卖光，客户在考虑性能更低的芯片\"；\"旧型号 GPU 的租金在涨\"；研究机构说\"这让 Rubin Ultra 暂时没有成熟的扩展方案，给对手留出了空间\"；\"华为现在占中国 AI 芯片市场约一半，这个市场约 500 亿美元\"。\n有数字也要过上面的规则：\n作者说\"英特尔多年领先，占 CPU 市场 59%\"，和英伟达的问题关系不大，分析员不会记。\n5.2 不完整也是证据\n预测、传闻、反证、远期、条件情景都是证据；它们的层级和确定性另记。不因为\"还没发生\"\"不是官方\"\"离现在远\"\"和我们方向相反\"就不留；一个真实的反例可能比十个顺风数字更重要\n第 1 版 §2\n。有名有姓的动作缺金额、缺日期也是证据，分量可以低一点\nD240\n。不留清单只按 5.3 各行和边界删，不能用来删掉标准要留的陈述：\"a reject rule never removes what the standard keeps\"\nD240\n。\n5.3 不留的\n第 1 版 §2 的清单，加 D238、D243 的行\n\"按同一行补的例子\"一列是我按同一行举的例子。\n不留\n第 1 版的例子\n按同一行补的例子\n边界\n没有业务规模的产品、游戏、驱动、跑分细节\n\"这张卡 415W\"；\"Geekbench 跑分\"\n接口、功率、旧型号的支持周期\n对手拿它和本公司产品比的说法不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判（Alex 判 4：AMD 2.4 倍是辅助）；伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不是产品细节（5.1 第 2 类）；公司自己、伙伴或第三方发布的对比（\"比闭源模型便宜 10 倍\"、\"同样功率多放 40% GPU\"、第三方测试）是产品细节，不收；同一句里伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不因这一条删，照旧按 5.1 判\nD250 第 3 条\n股价涨跌、投资人仓位、技术面\n\"本月跌 13%\"；\"Burry 做空\"\n期权成交、基金买卖\n股价反应背后的那条新闻不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判；\"财报后股价反应\"可选\n第 1 版 §10.2\n；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选\nD243 第 4 条\n；别的股价涨跌照旧不留\n作者的一般性风险清单和没有推导的看法\n\"如果 AI 退潮会受伤\"\n\"这只股票该持有\"；\"芯片指数是纯粹的高估\"\n有假设、有推导、有时点的条件估值要留\n第 1 版 §10.1\n；具名分析师对一件已记下的事的解读（\"推迟会给对手留出窗口\"），没有新事实或推算的，不收，有新事实或推算的按 5.1 判\nD250 第 4 条\n记者的背景和框架\n\"A100 2020 年上市\"；\"海湾国家花了几十亿但仍依赖它\"\n\"公司成立于 1998 年\"；通用的技术讲解\n没有数字和具体叙事的主张和背景不收；怎么分见 5.1\nD243 第 5 条\n；只描述一个现成安排（合作、客户在用、已有部署）、没有新动作的，是背景，不收，文中没写日期也一样\nD250 第 1 条\n没有数字也没有决定的政治、战略表态\n\"分散供应商不能减少政治依赖\"\n大使馆说\"立场一贯\"\n官员宣布的政策步骤不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判（第 4 类）\n同一事实的复述\n一篇稿子用五句话说一个部署是一条；三家媒体转同一个独家，原始出处写同一个\n标题和正文说同一件事\n已知事实的重述\n两个月后再说一遍 Q1 收入、指引、已公布的回购\n抽取看不到库，只能从文字和日期看。重提的过去实际数可选；重提的季度指引和仍然有效的预测、计划按本来分量；看不出是旧事的不算重提\nD243 第 6 条\n；只描述现成安排、没有新动作的，按背景判（见\"记者的背景和框架\"一行），其余仍按这一行判\nD250 第 1 条\n模板\n署名、作者简介、经纪商页脚、前瞻性声明、广告\n页面上和本文无关的内容（相关文章预告、推荐列表、别的新闻标题）\n只有形容的引语\n\"a pivotal moment\"；\"heterogeneous inference\"（role-v18 原文的例子）\nD243 第 10 条\n新加坡转运案件的概况和被告个人资产\n可选，收不收都不扣分\nD231\n另外一条来自后来的决定：\n和公司所有市场都无关的别家公司的估值、股价、交易\n不留（D238：\"another company's valuation, share price or deals that touch none of the company's markets\"）。别家公司的动作和经营数字不能用这一行删掉，它们留不留按 5.1 的规则判：要说得出它通过需求、供给、竞争、定价这类路径动了在研公司的哪个问题；只因同一篇文章、同一场活动或同一个生态挨着、说不出这条路径的，不收\nD250 第 2 条\n。\n不在清单上的句子，用 5.1 的规则逐句判（分析员会不会记）；金标里把这类判断标出来供复核。\n5.4 一条记录必须保住的意思\n第 1 版 §3，原样\n六格里只填这一条用得上的\nD220\n。\n格\n要保住\n丢了就错的例子\n谁\n原始判断者；是本人估计还是别人实测\nAMD 自己的模拟说成独立实测\n什么\n业务、指标或动作\n\"收入增长 82%\"丢了\"收入\"\n多少\n金额、比例、数量\n四家 7450 亿写成五家\n哪个期间\n预测或事实覆盖的期间；\"今年\"按公司财年或自然年写都算对\nD233\n，余下时间不改成全年\n\"今年余下时间\"改成 FY2027 全年\n什么条件\n推导的假设；计划、上限、\"最多\"\n\"最多 4 万颗\"写成已部署 4 万颗\n预测还是已发生\n指引、预计、商谈、传闻不等于已实现、已签约\n\"商谈 6000 亿\"写成\"已承诺\"\n原始发言日不知道就写不知道，不能填报道日\nD220\n。一条记录单独读要能懂，条件不能只放在旁边另一条里\n第 1 版 §3，引主分支 D251；两个分支的决定编号各自独立，D227\n。不必要的：转述机构、普通发言场合、付费方案全名；漏了不扣原意分，\n编造了仍算记录错误\n第 1 版 §3\n。没有来源证实的结论，可以作为分析员的推断写出，但不能写成来源说的事实\nD218\n。\n边界\n第 1 版 §2\n：Sharon 合同是供需的辅助证据，不是担保金额的证据；NVL72x2 取消归产品进度\nAlex 判 10\n。\n5.5 来源层\nPRD 06 的四层；D238 采纳的 role-v29 原文\nPRD 06 的定义：事实＝正式动作、公布的数字、完成的交易；当事人陈述＝公司指引、管理层表态、官员讲话；第三方观点＝卖方分析师、媒体分析、专家；传闻＝匿名来源的说法和没有出处的说法。层级跟着\"是谁确认的\"走，没有固定分数；二十家转载一个独家是一个来源。\nrole-v29 提示词的原文（D238；Alex 在 D239 选它作为抽取请求；D245 换成的 role-v42、D246 换成的 role-v51、D247 换成的 role-v53 这一句都未改）：\"fact for official actions, published actual figures or completed transactions; party_statement for company guidance, management statements or officials' remarks; third_party_view for analyst, media or expert opinions; rumour for claims the item credits to unnamed sources or calls unconfirmed; an announcement or completed deal the item reports is a fact even when the reporter names no source.\"\n这两处列了四层，下表把它们排成一张。一句话同时符合两行时，取表里上面那行\nD243 第 9 条\n。\n说的是什么\n层\n1. 文章说来自没具名的人，或文章自己说未经证实（即使说的是一项公告或一个数）\n传闻\n2. 正式动作、公布的实际数字、完成的交易；文章报道的公告或已完成的交易（即使记者没写出处）\n事实\n3. 公司的指引、管理层的表态、官员的讲话\n当事人陈述\n4. 分析师、媒体、专家的看法和估计\n第三方观点\n5. 以上都不是、又没有出处的说法（PRD 06：\"unattributed statements\"）\n传闻\n一条记录里既有已发生的部分、又有计划或传闻的细节，按主要说法定层，次要部分的性质写进摘要；不拆成两条，除非是两件事\nD243 第 9 条\n。层级之间的关系：后来的确认和先前的传闻都留在账本里；匹配判断两条的关系（同一说法、修订或新的），修订的条件过了才记修订，没过就作为新的一条存下\nD202、D204\n；同一说法在判断时怎么显示，见第 2 节\nD248 第 7 条\n；判断时只有事实能确定到期落在哪一档\nPRD 06\n。\n6\n分量\n分量问\"这条对判断多重要\"，分四级。按投资后果判它碰到的是什么；核心和辅助的界线按信息量定\n第 1 版 §4；D243 第 1 条\n。缺金额、缺日期只影响分到哪一档，不是不留的理由\nD240\n。分量不由数字多不多、是不是官方、库里有没有页、和我们方向是否相同、文章篇幅决定\n第 1 版 §4\n；离现在远不是不留的理由\nD216\n，它落在核心还是辅助由判断定。\n等级\n什么样\n例子\n核心\n碰到关键判断，并且带着能定位答案的信息。关键判断：收入、利润、融资或信用风险、需求、关键产品进度、关键投入的供给和价格；相对在研公司判，包括它的约束性义务和它承载价值的那一代产品及其配置、过渡方案。能定位答案的信息：一个量（金额、比例、数量；在谈的金额也算，\"在谈\"写进意思里）、一个确定状态，或一个带期间的预测。对公司不是小事，也不是别家公司自己的经营数字\n分析师预计今年余下收入增长 82%、2027 年 41%；云巨头 2026 年合计支出 6500 亿美元；内存厂说短缺持续到 2030；为客户 1050 亿美元租约担保；公司宣布为云客户产能兜底的新计划（确定状态，没给金额）；银行指引全年净利息收入降 3–5%；监管机构批准主力药或定下裁决日；主要供应商宣布 9 月起涨价 8%；交易对手披露公司 34 亿美元的采购承诺；据报道在谈最多 6000 亿美元的支持\n辅助\n支持或反驳上面的判断，或给出可检验的估值假设；碰到关键判断但没有能定位答案的信息；别家公司自己的经营数字（收入、利润率、积压订单）；对公司规模来说是小事的；离现在越远、判断下来只是辅助的预测\n对手自己模拟的 2.4 倍性能比较；公司取消软件订阅费；三条开放模型政策表态；Sharon 六年 72 MW 合同；FY2028 EPS 一致预期；12.76 × 25 的条件估值；一家银行选用对手系统（没写金额）；伙伴把公司模型集成进政府系统（没写金额）；内存厂公布的季度收入和利润率；客户公布的订单积压；客户园区\"规划最多 17 万颗 GPU\"\n可选\n帮助理解市场预期，省略不损害论证；留不留都不扣分\n财报后股价反应；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价；生态数字；重提的过去实际数；新加坡案件\nD231、D243 第 3、4、6 条\n不收\n第 5.3 节的各行；与判断无关\n模板；产品参数；个人仓位；空泛风险；没有数字和具体叙事的主张\n判法\n先判留不留\n按 5.1 判分析员会不会记，不会就不收。在 5.3 的不留清单上的，先看那一行的\"边界\"一列：写着\"不能用这一行删掉\"或\"按 5.1 判\"的（对手比较、伙伴或客户的采用或集成、股价反应背后的新闻、官员宣布的政策步骤、带来新事实或推算的解读）按 5.1 判，留下的按下面两步定分量；写着\"要留\"或\"按本来分量\"的（条件估值、重提的指引和仍然有效的预测、计划）照办；写着可选的归可选；其余不收。\n是可选的那几样吗？\n在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价，财报后股价反应，生态数字，重提的过去实际数，新加坡案件，归可选。带推导的条件估值例外，是辅助\n第 1 版 §10.1\n。\n核心还是辅助\n碰到关键判断、带着能定位答案的信息、对公司不是小事、不是别家公司自己的经营数字，四条都满足是核心；其余是辅助。别家公司直接说出关键投入或需求的状况（短缺到哪年、明年花多少），按关键判断算。预测离现在越远，越可能只是辅助，这由判断定：FY2028 EPS 一致预期是辅助，Alex 说\"太久远了\"；内存厂说短缺 2027 年最严重、持续到 2030 是核心，它覆盖今明两年。\n\"确定状态\"\n：已经签了、宣布了、开始了、批准了、取消了、推迟到某个日期了、定下了裁决日；没有量的\"在谈\"\"在考虑\"\"可能\"\"在探索\"\"打算\"不算。确定状态以陈述当天为时间，不要求生效日。\n\"带期间的预测\"\n：分析师预计今年余下收入增长 82%；内存厂说短缺持续到 2030 年。确定不确定看说法本身的内容；靠不靠得住另记在来源层。\nAlex 拍过的分量\nD215；gold-check.md，D229\n：收入增速核心；内存短缺核心；AMD 跑分辅助；三条政策辅助；云厂商支出核心；Omniverse 辅助；Sharon \"辅助属于供给需求\"；商谈最多 6000 亿支持 OpenAI 核心（在谈的金额）；投资 SB Energy 15 亿核心；Broadcom AI 半导体收入 +143%、Google Cloud 三个数辅助（别家公司的经营数字）；FY2028 EPS 辅助（远期）；逐案审核、有拒绝辅助（没有量和确定结果）。\n分量只在评估时判。\n分量只在金标和测量里判，不进账本，不进抽取的输出\nD240\n；账本只存来源层。抽取的模型决定留不留、怎么写，以及提哪些候选问题\n第 1 版 §1\n。\n仿真里剩下的分歧几乎都在核心和辅助这条线上：\"对公司规模来说是不是小事\"从一句话里常常看不出来（第 12 节，第三轮）。所以核心和辅助分开报，评估时允许读者间合理的不同判\nD226、D228\n。\n7\n研究观点标准\n判断这一步归 PRD 06、D198、D201 管；这里只列和抽取共用的。\n观点是什么：\n一个关于业务和世界的、有论证的看法；立场来自论证和市场已经定价的东西相比；报表行是它的证据，不是它的定义\nAGENTS §8\n。\n判断读什么：\n一个问题的判断看到的，是它仍然有效的证据里的两部分：新到的（在它倒数第五次判断之后挂上的；判断不满五次时全部显示）和依据（最近十次判断里被引用过的）。其余仍在账本里有效，请求里写明还有多少条没显示；答案只能引用和推动显示了的\nD198；从 judging-v5 起，v4 的配置照旧读全部有效证据\n。依赖问题的结论和理由、标的问题的主题块也读\nPRD 06、D195\n。主题结论变了，同一轮里排队重判符合条件的标的问题；主题的结论不作为证据引用\nD195\n。\n研究的交付物是论证：\n保留不可改的来源记录，和一份有版本的研究论证，写明支持和反对的来源、是否独立、变化和局限\nD201\n。\n研究用途：\n评估判断时，读者按四级看它引用的每条证据对那个具体结论撑得住多强：直接可用、有条件可用、线索、不可用；同一条证据对不同结论可以不同\nD221\n。这是评估的读法，不是判断这一步要输出或存下的字段（D221：\"not a numerical confidence score or a new storage layer\"）。用途和分量不同：分量问\"这条多重要\"，用途问\"拿它证明这个结论够不够\"。\n事实和推断分开：\n来源没说的结论可以作为分析员推断写出，但要标明\nD218\n。只有事实能确定落在哪一档\nPRD 06\n。\n重复不是新证据，模型意见一致不是互相印证\nAGENTS §8\n。\n8\n金标：改提示词的测试标准\n下面这份第 3 版金标是历史记录。第 4 版（D248）改了开题的规则，没有改动证据条目（逐条核对见\nresearch/2026-10-30-noise/gold-v4-evidence-check.md\n）；第 5 版（D250）改了证据的四条界线。金标的证据部分按第 5 版重推，两位互不知情的读者逐条判，分歧对着原文定，另存成新文件冻结，每条改动写改前、改后和理由，第 3 版文件不动（D243 第 11 条）。开题部分要按第 4 版逐条重判，重判之前打分程序不报开题的分；证据部分版本对不上，打分程序拒绝打分（D244、D250）。\n第 5 版金标（2026-10-31 冻结）：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/golden-v5/golden-v5-dev.json\n（sha256\n6f9476bb6a81ea590af273a71565e7c3c0f49908d26274b8482d52cfdee31a21\n）和\ngolden-v5-test.json\n（sha256\naf2145cdc60c34ec1ded0bebcb809800e187d616992ea325919c3ad771da61e3\n）。重判了 537 条（全部要收、可收的证据，引 D238、别家公司、5.1 判断或新加坡案件的不收句，和盲读认为该留的 35 句）：Codex 和 Claude 两位读者各自盲读，96 条进裁定，62 处改动逐条写改前、改后、两位读者的判法和理由（\ngolden-v5/corrections-v5.json\n）。开发集：核心 86（不变）、辅助 151（原 181）、可选 72；不收 770、可选 31。测试集：核心 9、辅助 37（原 42）、可选 1。两篇以中东冲突主题为对象的文章，读者被告知按在研公司读，是说明写错，那几条不改。下面的数字是第 3 版的历史记录。\n按这一页从原文逐篇写的参照，用来量提示词改动。Alex 的要求是\"以上有你自己trace 过的golden set 来做为改prompt测试标准\"\nD241\n。定稿文件：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/golden-v3/golden-v3-signed.json\n，sha256\n78844bc329fc85000e18ddcab9ea59feb57abe6407cf11bbead4ba8fe4c3c0d3\nD243\n。\n118\n篇测试文章：那一周 98 篇，留出 20 篇\n348\n条要收或可以收的证据，一件事一条\n738\n句不收，各写理由；另有 24 句可选\n1110\n段原文片段，代码核过都在原文里\n28 · 42\n个必须开、可以开的问题；240 个不该开\n每一条记什么\n证据：\n原文逐字片段；属于 5.1 哪一类；定稿分量\nweight\n（核心、辅助、可选）；来源层；谁；必须保住的意思（5.4 用得上的格）；判它的那一句标准；签字前的两套分量和它依赖哪几条，留作记录。\n不收的句子：\n属于 5.3 哪一行，或按 5.1 的理由；\nweight\n是不收或可选。可选的留不留都不扣分。\n问题：\n必须开的（类、开题理由、标的或主题、要点、\nsettled_by\n）；可以开的（另一种合理写法）；不该开的（第 4 节哪一行或哪个测试）。\n没判清的地方：\n两种读法都写出来。\n分量的分布（定稿）\n那一组\n核心\n辅助\n可选\n要收或可收\n不收\n原列不收、现为可选\n那一周 98 篇\n80\n152\n63\n295\n621\n22\n留出 20 篇\n6\n29\n18\n53\n117\n2\n参考答案第 2 版那一周是核心 47、辅助 69、可选 48。辅助多出来的，主要是有名有姓、没写规模的动作（伙伴集成、客户采用、合作）：第 2 版标成\"什么都不留\"，现在按 D240 收为辅助。\n怎么写成、怎么核的\n六个起草者分批写\nClaude opus 各写 20 篇左右，只读标准和原文；参考答案第 2 版只当起点，每条重判。\n合并时统一写法\nmerge.py\n统一各批的写法，每处写理由（\nmerge-log.txt\n）：旧事重提 49 条逐条判；估值倍数和别家公司股价按现行规则处理；中国是否批准 H200 进口改为主题问题。\n逐条读改动\nClaude 读了每批相对参考答案第 2 版新增、删掉、改档的条目：新增约 124 条，改档约 37 条；旧条目没对上的 10 条逐条查过，都在，只是引文片段不同或按旧事重提处理。\n两轮独立盲读\nCodex 读者只读这份标准和原文，不看金标。读者多留的逐条对原文判：金标漏了的补上，其余写明不补的理由。补进来的 49 条，\"谁\"和\"意思\"对着原文补齐（\nadditions-fields.json\n）。\n按签字定稿\nfinalize.py\n按 D243 给每一条定分量，39 处改动逐条写理由（\nfinalize-log.txt\n）：32 条移出要收的一栏，其中 24 条是常识性的主张和背景（第 5 条），1 条是只有形容的引语（第 10 条），7 条和起草者写了理由的不收句是同一句；另有 7 句不收句和要收的条目重复，删掉。这 19 处\"同一句话既留又不留\"，是盲读后补条目时没和不收的句子对照造成的。此外，不收的句子里 24 句按第 3、4、6 条改为可选。\n盲读\n金标条目\n读者也留了\n分量相同\n读者多留\n补进金标\n第一轮：随机 15 篇，补之前\n34\n33\n26\n19\n8\n第一轮：补之后\n42\n41\n32\n11\n第二轮：其余 103 篇，补之前\n229\n194\n162\n144\n40\n第二轮：补之后\n262\n227\n193\n结论：起草稿大约漏了两成，集中在有名有姓、没写规模的采用和合作，和一些带期间的判断。补完以后，独立读者能找到的金标条目约九成。读者多留的 163 条里，48 条是金标漏了、已补；其余 115 条按标准不补：同一件事金标已有、新加坡案件、产品细节、泛泛看法、旧事重提、合作方自己的事、和公司市场无关、只描述长期状况。剩下的不一致集中在核心和辅助的界线（第 1 条）和\"背景还是主张\"（第 5 条），两条都已由 Alex 拍定。记录在\ngolden-v3/spotcheck.md\n、\npass2-decisions.json\n。\n用它之前要知道的\n它是模型写的。\nClaude 起草和裁定、Codex 盲查；按 AGENTS §7 这样标明。Alex 抽查之前，它不是 Alex 的判断。\n留出集要换。\n之前的提示词循环发过这 20 篇，答案读过一部分。按第 1 版 §11，\"看过模型答案的文章只做开发集\"，所以第一次改提示词之前，要另取一批没人看过答案的文章，从原文写参照，同样盲查、定稿、记 sha256。\n发现错了怎么办。\n不改这个文件；另记一份更正，每条写理由和改前改后，测量用更正后的版本并注明\nD243 第 11 条\n。\n按金标打分的程序还没写。\n代码只核结构和计数；意思由没写这次改动的读者判（第 9.2 节）。\n9\n改提示词、测量、迭代\n签字以后的第一件事：把现行提示词（role-v29）和这一页逐句对一遍，列出不一致的地方，再按 9.3 一类一类改。一般的规则全文在\nAGENTS.md\n§6（写提示词）和 §7（测量和迭代），这里只写到这一步怎么用，出处标在后面。\n9.1 改提示词\n每一句都对得上这一页。\n提示词里每一句关于留、不留、分量、开题的话，在这一页找得到出处；找不到的，是给 Alex 的提议，不是改动\nAGENTS §6\n。\n写\"不留\"之前先核它删不掉要留的。\n分析员会记的陈述，缺金额、缺日期本身不是不留的理由；分量另判，可能低一点\nD240\n。Alex 的原话：\"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？……你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\"。\n通用的对冲基金大脑。\n每一句、每个例子对银行、药企、零售、芯片读起来一样；例子跨行业，不点名覆盖的公司，不取自测试文章\nD237；AGENTS §6\n。\n不靠加长。\n读整个请求，就像模型读到的那样（系统提示、schema、内容）；列出矛盾、为别的情况写的规则、逼出缺陷的说法；删、并、拆。缺一句才加，别处删掉同样多；报改前改后的字数和 token\nAGENTS §6\n。\n分类写成封闭集合。\n每类两三个跨行业的例子，加走法（从第一段读到最后一段）和停止点（\"一页有八条就是八条\"）\nAGENTS §6\n。例子就是指令：和 schema、读答案的代码不一致的例子删掉。\n发之前先免费核一遍。\n写一份照提示词作答的假答案，过一遍全部代码检查；契约句逐条对金标该收和该拒的例子读一遍；确认发出去的字节和生产一样；\nscripts/preflight.py\n把存下的答案重放一遍\nAGENTS §5、§6\n。\n9.2 测量\n一把尺子。\n这一页 → 定稿金标 → 下面的达标线。另一把尺子在用，比如旧参考答案、评审打分表、提示词自己的类别，就是缺陷，先修掉再测\nD241；AGENTS §7\n。\n计数单位。\n一条\"不收\"的金标条目，只有带着它的那条记录本身不是金标判为核心、辅助或可选的同一件事时，才算留下；引文里顺带带出的不收半句，是那件事的同一条记录，不算误收。是不是同一件事，代码的计数辅助先算，有出入的由读者对着原文判\nD250 第 5 条；第 2 节\"一件事用几句话说仍是一条\"\n。\n不在还在改的尺子上给自己打分。\n金标先给 Alex 看、冻结，再开始改提示词；意思由没写这次改动的读者按金标判；代码只核结构和计数\nAGENTS §7\n。\n被扔的逐条读。\n按四种归因计数（第 2 节），从最大的一类修起\nD209\n。\n总数只看三次发送。\n一次发送只能说明机制；直接读（每篇发一次，每个答案和原文并排读，D238）用来找机制，它的数是一次发送的数，要这样标明。回归先报，每个数都报\nAGENTS §7\n。\n达标线是第 1 版 §11，逐字照抄在下面的引线内：\n单位\n：一篇原文里的一个研究事项（可由多条证据表达）。参照在任何发送之前写好并冻结（sha256），从原文写，不看模型答案；看过模型答案的文章只做开发集，留出集另取没看过的文章。\n每个参照项记四件事\n（gold v5 §二）：选了没有（kept / omitted，拆几条合几条只算一次）；原意（correct / partial / wrong / absent）；可核查（引文和上下文够不够）；记录问题（原始日期、期间、归属、确定性、错来源、重复、推断未标明，各标 material / record_only）。\n分开报，不合成总分\n：核心 N 项里 correct / partial / wrong / absent 各多少；辅助同样；误收率 = 留下的\"不收\"项 ÷ 评过的\"不收\"项；多留的垃圾按条数（我读，代码核结构）；每个 material 问题单列。分母为零报 not_exercised。三次独立发送逐次报，不投票。\n达标线（提案）\n：\n核心：每一次发送都 kept 且 correct，不带 material 记录问题（v18 验收用的就是\"六篇五发每条都在\"）。\n辅助：三次里至少两次 kept，correct 或 partial。\n不收：模板 0/3 留下（新加坡案件按 D231 改为可选）。\n垃圾：每篇留下的无关陈述不多于 Luna 对照组的中位数（基线，不是目标）。\n候选：§6 例子里该开的一个问题在每次发送都提出，且 settled_by 写得出；不该开的（部署计划、季度指引、同段每行）0 次。\n不达标就读原始答案找机制，再改一处；不在读过的文章上调。\n结构由代码查\n（免费）：schema 通过、引文逐字在原文里、每条一个陈述、候选的 due 在发布日之后。结构错不算内容错，分开报。\n引文检查接受的写法：只在标点、引号、大小写上和原文不同，或在一句之内省略一段，存原文\nD236\n。\n之后的决定对它的影响：参考答案第 2 版里\"整篇什么都不留\"的标注不再当目标\nD240\n；\"垃圾\"一项用定稿金标重算，Luna 基线也重算\nD243 第 12 条\n；第 1 版说的\"§6 例子里该开的一个问题\"是 AI Compute Partnership 的义务金额题；合理的不同判和不同措辞可以接受，可选细节和标签不同不变成硬性的过或不过\nD226、D228\n。\n9.3 迭代：一类一类改\nAlex：\"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"。每一轮是下面六步：\n挑最大的一类\n把漏收、误收和被扔的按机制归类计数，从最大的一类开始；报这一类占多少\nAGENTS §1\n。\n找到是哪一句\n读这一类的原始答案，和原文并排（改之前一共至少读过十个原始答案）；对模型做出的选择，接着那次对话问它\"哪一句让你这样判\"；先看请求里有没有它需要的、schema 写不写得出\nAGENTS §1、§6\n。\n只改一处\n一次改一句或一组说同一件事的话，别处删掉同样多；新的话对得上这一页\nAGENTS §6\n。\n全部文章各发一次，和上一版比\n改一句会改变每一次请求，所以要发的是全部文章。\"别的版本都留住、只有这一版丢了\"的核心或辅助才算回归信号；单次发送本身每版有 0–4 条这样的意外丢失\nD243 第 8 条；surprise.py\n。每个信号读原始答案、问模型自己的理由，追到是不是这次改的那一句造成的。这是诊断。出错的那几篇只用来找机制；接不接受，看全部文章三次发送、和新对照一起报的结果\nAGENTS §7\n。\n记一行\n假设、改动、数字（回归先报）、留不留；没用的改动和自己的错也记\nAGENTS §7\n。\n一批改完再验收\n在最终配置上三次发送，按 9.2 由不是作者的读者评，结果给 Alex\n第 1 版 §11\n。\n尺子有疑问就停。\n改的时候发现金标和这一页说法不同、两份标准打架、或读者对\"什么是对的\"有分歧，就停下来先和 Alex 定尺子，不在有疑问的尺子上接着调\nD241；AGENTS §7\n。\n10\nAlex 已经拍过的判例\n第 1 版 §8 的全部，加之后的。\n日期\n判例\n原话或答案\n10-22\n讲话方式\n\"explain and answer like I'm 16 and in human understandable language\"\nAGENTS §8\n观点是什么\nA thesis is an argued view about the business and the world; its stance comes from the argument weighed against what is priced. Financial-statement lines are its evidence, never its definition.\n10-26 D191\n共同量、主题\n决定文字：\"\nindustry_aggregate\nis renamed\nshared_quantity\n: a quantity or price outside any one company that several move on\"，Alex：\"好\"；纯宏观文章只在主题在名单上时开，Alex：\"是\"；主题对公司一对全部，Alex：\"我觉得这个 1 to all mapping 比较 make sense\"\n10-26 D187\n别家公司的量\n选 C（\"可以\"）：任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算；记录，未实现\n10-27\n这一步的标准\n\"做一个 hedge fund analyst 的专业视角去看怎么做这一步的标准\"；\"什么叫重要陈述，你也没给例子\"\n10-28 D208\n驱动层\n\"可以，但是不能因为有了这层我们的抽取就是非常的模版化缺少了hedge fund analyst 的灵魂\"；宏观\"算是个global factor to all, 但影响正反面不一定\"\n10-28 D209\n没家的陈述\n\"if it doesn't matter, just fucking throw it away … if there are false positives that got thrown away … you got to pick it up. Again, that's the way to do proper evaluation.\"\n10-28\n不许前视\n\"不是你这么粗暴的把这些都先写好那岂不是lookahead了\"\n10-28\n逐条读\n\"有用的，你得一条一条看是不是真的值得抽才知道你这些改动的抽取效果\"\n10-29 D215\n十一题\n1 A（收入增速 核心）；2 C→D216 \"2028 EPS 预测可以留下来啊，我的意思是因为太久远了，现在才2026\"；3 A（内存短缺 核心）；4 B（AMD 跑分 辅助）；5 C（新加坡案件 不留，D231 改为可选）；6 B（三条政策 辅助）；7 A（云厂商支出 核心）；8 B（Omniverse 辅助）；9 \"辅助属于供给需求\"（Sharon）；10 \"属于产品进度问题\"（NVL72x2）；11 \"一条吧都属于财务\"（Google Cloud 三个数一条）\n10-29 D218\n推断\n\"是推断，你不要再问我了，你要自己尝试 figure out 开始跑\"\n10-29 D220\n谁定标准\n\"你来修正对齐，你是 hedge fund analyst 你来做好标准\"\n10-29 D217\n目标模型\n\"现在开始以haiku 为模型取代luna … 每一轮都根据尺子去迭代 … 小心 overfit … 尺子其实不对 … scope creep\"（D234 起 Haiku 暂停）\n10-29 D226、D228\n评法\n合理的模糊和不同措辞可以接受；金标例子教的是判断逻辑，不是唯一答案；两套既有标准（证据层次、90 题评审）都要用；来源、用途、准入分开判\n10-29\n今天\n\"之前没有对齐一个hedge fund analyst 应该要用什么标准来抽研究观点\"\n10-29 D229\n标准不漂移\n\"1-5 都可以，按照这套标准来开始做测量，这条标准不要漂移\"\n10-29 D231\n新加坡\n\"新加坡那条就随意吧\"\n10-29 D233\n今年\n按公司财年或自然年写都算对\n10-29 D236\n三条提议\n\"好都按照你的提议来。但是你的prompt 改动要确认不是hardcode for nvidia 你在建构的是hedge fund brain\"\n10-29 D237\n通用\n\"It needs to be general purpose as a hedge fund brain\"\n10-29 D238\n直接读\n\"你根本不要再给我跑什么三轮了。每一篇的问题明显地出错的你不要再用工具尺子了，你直接下去看\"\n10-29 D240\n不完整的证据\n\"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？这个证据显然是有意义的，不一定什么条件数据时间都要满足，你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\"\n10-29 D241\n这份标准\n\"几个大类型的账本 - 开题标准和档次 这两个要完整的定义好写好\"；\"档位根据信息量作定标 -以上有你自己trace 过的golden set 来做为改prompt测试标准\"\n10-29 D243\n签字\n第 5 条：\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"；方向：\"不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎不要看什么都是线索的感觉，但是同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过\"；\"其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off\"\n10-29 D242\n这一页\n\"你给我一个网页，这个同时也是你这个环节重要的PRD\"\n10-30 D248\n颗粒度\n七条各选 A（第 6 条\"A， A\"）；原则：\"我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息，这个是这个步骤的原则\"\n11\n在用的标准之间的冲突，怎么摆平\n编号对应盘点报告\ninventory-2026-10-29.md\n的 B1–B20，B21 是后来加的。\"按已拍的\"是已有决定，只是没落实或没写全；文首 12 条的决定记在 D243；\"我定\"只限措辞和记录约定。\n#\n冲突\n处理\n依据\nB1\n共同量的名字和定义：D191 改名并扩到价格；v16 起提示词和代码用\nindustry_aggregate\n、几家公司合计、一个日历年\n按 D191 落实为\nshared_quantity\n，期间仍用自然年；提示词和代码要跟着改\nD191、D243；PRD 06\nB2\n主驱动量含不含产品节点\n含，写成 A、B 两类\nD236（在 D184 之后）\nB3\n约束性义务：一笔或一个计划一题（D210），还是一组入股是一个判断（D201 规则 G）\n按 D210 \"一笔或一个计划\"；一笔交易属不属于已开的计划，由匹配判；同一计划的几种说法是一题；够不上公司规模的单笔交易不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD210、D236、D238、D248\nB4\n开题测试三套，提示词和代码都没用\n第 1 版 §6 四个测试加评审三条写法检查（第 4 节）；测试 4 和库比的部分归匹配\n第 1 版 §6；评审 §1；D211\nB5\nPRD 05 还写着\nfolds_into\n、第 5 块\n抽取不看库；PRD 05 过时段落签字后改指这一页\nD211\nB6\n没家的证据扔不扔\n产品里扔，评估读被扔的、分四种归因；两者不冲突\nD209；第 1 版 §7\nB7\n归档规则三个版本\n以 D207 为准；归档提示里点名 NVIDIA 的那句按 D237 改成通用（后续一步）\nD207、D237\nB8\n分量几级、\"档位\"一词两义\n四级；\"档位\"在这一页叫分量，问题的叫结果档\n第 1 版 §10.3；D243 第 1 条\nB9\n几套\"不要\"的类\n抽取只有一套：按 5.3 各行及其边界判，判为不收的分量是\"不收\"；\"不可用\"属于判断这一步（第 7 节）；\"无关陈述\"用定稿金标计数\n第 1 版 §2、§4；D221；D240；D243 第 12 条\nB10\n股价、目标价、重述、新加坡各处判得不同\n财报后股价反应可选（第 1 版 §10.2）；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选；重提的过去实际数可选；新加坡可选\n第 1 版 §10.2；D231；D243 第 4、6 条\nB11\n主题怎么连到公司\n一对全部的主题块；文章明写的依赖照记；D208 的通道未建\nD191、D195\nB12\n研究组、驱动层\n研究组不是问题，不代替开题判断；七类驱动是重要性的理由；词汇表只禁 \"thesis driver\"\nD165、D208；评审 §6\nB13\nPRD 06 的重复和替代说法早于 D202、D204\n这一页按 D202、D204 写（第 2 节、5.5）；PRD 06 签字后更新\nD202、D204\nB14\n组成部分算不算同一题\n同一总量的另一种人群或口径是同一题；有自己驱动、答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的是另一题，要过独立意义测试\nD190；第 1 版 §6；D248\nB15\n标的五档必须叫\"非常看多……\"（代码），还是写业务结果（D186）\n这一页按 D186 写；代码是另一模块，D186 未实现照旧记\nD186\nB16\n转述的公告算事实还是当事人陈述\n事实（role-v29 原文：\"an announcement or completed deal the item reports is a fact even when the reporter names no source\"）；混合的一条按主要说法定层\nPRD 06；D238、D239；D243 第 9 条\nB17\n代码按自然年拒公司明年的财年量\n已修：年限按公司财年数，测试\ntests/test_fiscal_year_horizon.py\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D243\nB18\n\"线索\"\"背景\"一词多义\n\"线索\"只指研究用途的一级（第 7 节）；\"背景\"只指 5.3 那一行\nD221\nB19\n谁把候选并进已开问题\n匹配和碰撞裁决；共同量由代码\nD190、D197、D211\nB20\nAGENTS §8 指向两套标准，D229 又说第 1 版是唯一尺子\n这一页签字后取代第 1 版，AGENTS §8 指向这一页；对两套既有标准（PRD 06 证据层次、90 题评审）的指向保留，它们是这一页的出处\nD228、D229、D243\nB21\nPRD 06 State 写判断要 \"reconsider all evidence still in force\"，D198 之后只显示新到的和依据\n按 D198（在后）；PRD 06 签字后更新\nD198\n12\n校验记录和出处\n三轮独立读者仿真\n每一轮把标准和一组事先定死的案例交给互相看不到答案的读者：作者先写好自己的答案并记 sha256；Claude 的不同模型和 Codex 各读一份。读者只读标准和案例，不读决策、金标、提示词。几方不一致的案例，读原因，追到标准里的一句话，改那一句；下一轮换没见过的读者，加没见过的案例。共 192 个陈述、40 个问题；陈述里 152 个来自测试文章，40 个来自银行、药企、零售、航空、公用事业、矿业、保险等其他行业。\n第三轮，一对读者\n新案例：留不留\n新案例：连分量\n难案例复核：留不留\n新问题\n作者–Codex\n96%\n87%\n84%\n5/8\n作者–Claude sonnet\n94%\n83%\n91%\n6/8\n作者–Claude fable\n93%\n81%\n89%\n6/8\nCodex–sonnet\n94%\n83%\n86%\n7/8\nCodex–fable\n93%\n83%\n84%\n7/8\nsonnet–fable\n98%\n89%\n95%\n8/8\n新案例上留不留的一致率从第二轮的 80–94% 升到 93–98%，连分量从 72–87% 升到 81–89%。剩下的分歧几乎都在核心和辅助的界线，由 D243 第 1 条定下判法。三位读者判对、作者判错的，记作作者的错：最大客户带来的收入和中国销售占比不该开题（独立意义测试），还有两处陈述。记录在\nsimulation-log.md\n。\n十一轮漂移检查\nCodex 只读，逐条对照签过的第 1 版、全部决策、PRD 06，找\"作者自己加的规则\"、和决策矛盾的地方、引错的原话。每一轮的发现都改掉并记下处理。第一轮查出 18 处自己加的规则和 9 处和决策矛盾，比如\"名单外公司的量不开题\"违反 D155、D187，\"抽取要和库里比重复\"违反 D211，把达标线缩短，把\"在谈的金额\"排除出核心。最后三轮只剩措辞层面的问题；第十一轮的发现在 2.14 版改掉，没有再复查。\n36\n2.3\n约20\n2.4\n约10\n2.5\n6\n2.6\n8\n2.7\n7\n2.8\n7\n2.9\n6\n2.10\n5\n2.11\n6\n2.12\n5\n2.13\n被检查的版本；颜色浅的两根是约数\n出处\nPRD 06（Two thesis kinds、Ladder、Four evidence tiers、State、Succession）；PRD 05；第 1 版标准（D229）；\nresearch_notes/对冲基金论点粒度与金标/codex-challenger.md\n§1 八条和 §6、\nindustry-debate-standards.md\n（主分支）；决定 D155、D165、D184、D185、D186、D187、D190、D191、D195、D197、D198、D199、D201、D202、D204、D207、D208、D209、D210、D211、D215、D216、D218、D220、D221、D226、D228、D229、D231、D233、D236、D237、D238、D240、D241、D242。\n文件\n这一页：\ndocs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\n。改它之前先在\ndocs/DECISIONS.md\n记下 Alex 的原话；改完跑一次只读的漂移检查。\n起草过程和证据：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/\n：\nstandard-v2.md\n到\nstandard-v2.14.md\n、\nsimulation-log.md\n、\nreview-drift-v2.*.md\n、\ninventory-2026-10-29.md\n、\ngolden-v3/\n。\n定稿金标：\ngolden-v3/golden-v3-signed.json\n（sha256 见第 8 节），由\ngolden-v3/finalize.py\n从起草稿定稿。\n第 1 版：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md\n（D229），已由这一页取代，留作出处。\nHivemind · 抽取与开题标准 · 第 5 版 · 2026-10-29 Alex 签字（D243），2026-10-30 按 Alex 逐个选的七条修订（D248），2026-10-31 按 Alex 定的证据四条界线和计数单位修订（D250）。页首的 standard-version 是规则的版本号：改任何一条规则都要升它，金标随之重定，打分程序遇到版本对不上就拒绝打分（D244）。\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
